WEBVTT

02:05.834 --> 02:06.835
<i>All right, team,</i>

02:06.918 --> 02:08.045
<i>stay in sight of each other.</i>

02:08.170 --> 02:09.254
<i>Let's make NASA proud today.</i>

02:10.672 --> 02:12.382
<i>How's it looking
over there, Watney?</i>

02:12.466 --> 02:13.925
<i>Well,
you'll be happy to hear...</i>

02:14.009 --> 02:16.720
<i>that in Grid Section 14-28,</i>

02:16.803 --> 02:19.723
<i>the particles were
predominantly coarse...</i>

02:19.806 --> 02:23.018
but in 29,
they're much finer...

02:23.185 --> 02:25.103
and they should be ideal
for chem analysis.

02:25.270 --> 02:26.563
<i>Oh, wow.
Did everybody hear that?</i>

02:26.647 --> 02:28.440
<i>Mark just discovered dirt.</i>

02:28.607 --> 02:29.650
<i>Should we
alert the media?</i>

02:29.816 --> 02:31.652
<i>Sorry, what are you
doing today, Martinez?</i>

02:31.818 --> 02:33.695
Making sure the MAV
is still upright?

02:33.779 --> 02:34.780
<i>I'd like you to know</i>

02:34.863 --> 02:35.906
<i>that visual inspection
of the equipment...</i>

02:35.989 --> 02:37.616
<i>is imperative
to mission success.</i>

02:37.699 --> 02:40.535
<i>I also would like to report
that the MAV is still upright.</i>

02:40.702 --> 02:41.828
<i>Watney,</i>

02:41.912 --> 02:43.163
<i>you keep leaving
your channel open...</i>

02:43.246 --> 02:45.040
<i>which leads to
Martinez responding...</i>

02:45.207 --> 02:46.458
<i>which leads to
all of us listening...</i>

02:46.625 --> 02:47.793
<i>which leads to me
being annoyed.</i>

02:48.418 --> 02:49.461
<i>Roger that.</i>

02:49.544 --> 02:50.837
<i>Martinez, the captain
would like you to please...</i>

02:51.004 --> 02:52.255
shut your smart mouth.

02:53.715 --> 02:55.217
We'd prefer to use
a different adjective

02:55.300 --> 02:56.968
to describe Martinez's mouth.

02:57.386 --> 02:58.762
<i>Did Beck
just insult me?</i>

02:58.929 --> 03:00.931
<i>"Dr. Beck. " And yes.</i>

03:01.098 --> 03:03.600
Happy to turn the radios off
from here, Commander.

03:03.767 --> 03:04.851
Just say the word.

03:05.018 --> 03:06.186
Wait, Johanssen.

03:06.269 --> 03:07.562
Constant communication
is the hallmark...

03:07.729 --> 03:08.772
Shut 'em off.

03:08.939 --> 03:09.981
<i>No.</i>

03:10.148 --> 03:11.316
No. Excuse me.

03:12.275 --> 03:14.236
<i>I apologize
for my countrymen, Vogel.</i>

03:14.486 --> 03:15.696
Accepted.

03:15.862 --> 03:17.823
How many samples
do we need, Commander?

03:18.031 --> 03:19.032
<i>Seven.</i>

03:19.533 --> 03:20.659
<i>100 grams each.</i>

03:26.998 --> 03:28.917
We have a mission update.
A storm warning.

03:29.084 --> 03:30.544
<i>Commander,
you should come inside.</i>

03:30.627 --> 03:31.920
<i>You're gonna want to see this.</i>

03:32.087 --> 03:33.088
What is it?

03:33.171 --> 03:34.214
<i>A storm warning.</i>

03:34.381 --> 03:35.716
I saw that
in this morning's briefing.

03:35.882 --> 03:37.759
We'll be inside
before it hits.

03:37.926 --> 03:40.637
Yeah, they upgraded
their estimate.

03:40.804 --> 03:42.431
The storm's gonna be
a lot worse.

03:48.937 --> 03:51.064
<i>Martinez,
how does it look?</i>

03:52.190 --> 03:53.275
<i>Not good.</i>

03:56.153 --> 03:59.114
"1,200 kilometers in diameter,
bearing 24.41 degrees."

03:59.281 --> 04:00.449
That's tracking
right towards us.

04:00.615 --> 04:01.992
"Based on current escalation,

04:02.075 --> 04:04.453
"estimated force
of 8,600 newtons."

04:05.370 --> 04:06.621
What's the abort force?

04:06.788 --> 04:08.457
7,500.

04:09.332 --> 04:11.585
<i>Anything more than
that and the MAV could tip.</i>

04:11.752 --> 04:13.044
Do we scrub?

04:13.211 --> 04:14.421
Begin abort procedure.

04:14.963 --> 04:17.257
We are estimating
with a margin of error.

04:17.424 --> 04:19.009
We could wait it out.

04:19.342 --> 04:20.844
Let's wait it out.

04:22.471 --> 04:23.680
Let's wait it out.

04:25.265 --> 04:26.725
Commander?

04:27.392 --> 04:28.685
Prep emergency departure.

04:28.852 --> 04:30.103
Commander?

04:30.270 --> 04:32.189
We're scrubbed.
That's an order.

04:42.741 --> 04:45.285
<i>Martinez,
how long before take-off?</i>

04:45.452 --> 04:46.495
12 minutes.

04:49.748 --> 04:51.416
Visibility
is almost zero.

04:51.583 --> 04:52.584
Anyone gets lost,

04:52.667 --> 04:53.877
hone in on
my suit's telemetry.

04:54.044 --> 04:55.045
You ready?

04:55.128 --> 04:56.379
Ready.

04:58.840 --> 05:00.217
Commander,
are you okay?

05:00.300 --> 05:01.593
I'm okay.

05:25.867 --> 05:27.244
Commander,
we're at 10 degrees,

05:27.327 --> 05:28.787
and the MAV
is gonna tip at 12.3.

05:29.412 --> 05:32.290
Hey! We might be able
to keep the MAV from tipping.

05:32.374 --> 05:33.416
How?

05:34.125 --> 05:37.128
Use the cables from the comms
mast as guy-lines...

05:37.295 --> 05:38.964
anchor it with the Rover's.

05:39.297 --> 05:40.340
Watch out!

05:43.593 --> 05:44.594
Watney!

05:46.263 --> 05:48.640
<i>Warning. Suit breach detected.</i>

05:48.765 --> 05:49.724
What happened?

05:49.808 --> 05:50.809
He was hit.

05:50.976 --> 05:51.977
Watney, report.

05:52.769 --> 05:53.770
Before we lost telemetry,

05:53.854 --> 05:54.896
his decompression alarm
went off.

05:55.021 --> 05:56.064
Where did you
last see him?

05:56.147 --> 05:57.232
I don't know where he is.

05:57.315 --> 05:58.316
What are the vitals
on his suit?

05:58.400 --> 05:59.401
He's offline.

05:59.484 --> 06:00.902
A complete loss
of signal on Watney.

06:01.069 --> 06:02.195
Beck!
Yeah.

06:02.362 --> 06:04.614
How long can he survive
decompression?

06:04.823 --> 06:06.074
Less than a minute.

06:06.533 --> 06:08.702
Line up, walk west.

06:08.869 --> 06:10.829
He may be prone.
We don't wanna step over him.

06:12.664 --> 06:13.874
Commander...

06:14.040 --> 06:15.667
We're at 10.5 degrees.

06:15.750 --> 06:16.918
<i>Warning. Excessive tilt.</i>

06:17.002 --> 06:19.796
Tilting to 11
with all the gusts of wind.

06:20.088 --> 06:21.339
Copy that.

06:21.506 --> 06:23.884
Everyone, hone in
on Martinez's suit.

06:24.050 --> 06:26.094
It'll get you to airlock.
Get in, prep for launch.

06:26.386 --> 06:27.762
What about you,
Commander?

06:27.929 --> 06:30.599
I'm gonna search
a little longer. Get moving!

06:30.932 --> 06:31.933
Go!

06:35.687 --> 06:36.813
Watney!

06:37.355 --> 06:38.940
Watney, report!

06:39.107 --> 06:41.151
<i>The MAV's
at 11.6 degrees.</i>

06:41.818 --> 06:43.194
One good gust
and we're tipping.

06:43.361 --> 06:44.571
If it tips, you launch.

06:44.738 --> 06:46.072
You really think
I'm gonna leave you behind?

06:46.239 --> 06:47.782
<i>That's an order,
Martinez.</i>

06:48.617 --> 06:50.452
Mark!

06:50.702 --> 06:53.747
Mark! Can you hear me?

06:55.290 --> 06:58.043
Martinez, what about
the proximity radar?

06:58.209 --> 06:59.878
Could that detect
Watney's suit?

07:00.045 --> 07:01.379
It's made to see
the <i>Hermes</i> from orbit...

07:01.546 --> 07:03.423
not a little piece of metal
from a single suit.

07:03.590 --> 07:04.591
<i>Give it a try.</i>

07:04.674 --> 07:05.675
Roger.

07:05.926 --> 07:06.927
What is she thinking?
She knows

07:07.010 --> 07:08.136
the infrared can't
get through a sandstorm.

07:08.303 --> 07:10.430
She's grasping
for anything.

07:10.597 --> 07:11.598
<i>We've got
negative contact</i>

07:11.681 --> 07:12.766
<i>on the proximity radar.</i>

07:12.891 --> 07:13.892
Nothing?

07:14.059 --> 07:16.019
<i>No.
I can barely see the Hab.</i>

07:17.145 --> 07:19.272
Commander, I know you don't
wanna hear this, but...

07:19.439 --> 07:20.523
Mark is dead.

07:22.025 --> 07:23.360
<i>Commander!</i>

07:23.526 --> 07:26.029
Hey, what the hell
is wrong with you, man?

07:26.196 --> 07:27.197
My friend just died.

07:27.280 --> 07:28.490
I don't want
my commander to die, too.

07:28.657 --> 07:29.866
<i>Stability warning.</i>

07:30.075 --> 07:31.117
We're tipping!

07:31.284 --> 07:34.579
<i>Commander, you need
to get back to the ship, now!</i>

07:34.663 --> 07:35.664
13 degrees.

07:36.623 --> 07:38.124
If we pass balance,
we'll never rock back.

07:38.291 --> 07:39.501
I've got
one more trick left,

07:39.584 --> 07:41.044
and then I'm following orders,
Commander.

07:42.921 --> 07:44.381
You're firing the OMS?

07:45.048 --> 07:46.049
That's right.

07:50.095 --> 07:51.179
Commander!

07:51.763 --> 07:53.056
On my way.

07:56.434 --> 07:57.644
<i>Johanssen, let's go.</i>

08:05.193 --> 08:07.237
Mark!

08:19.374 --> 08:20.834
<i>We're at 11.5
and holding.</i>

08:24.546 --> 08:25.588
<i>Ready to go on your command.</i>

08:28.049 --> 08:29.134
Ready to launch.

08:30.385 --> 08:31.636
Commander.

08:31.970 --> 08:33.638
I need you to verbally tell me
whether or not to.

08:33.805 --> 08:34.889
Launch.

08:34.889 --> 09:04.889
<b>Subtitle | www.movieddl.me</b>

09:16.473 --> 09:18.099
<i>At around 4:</i><i>30 a.m.</i>

09:18.183 --> 09:19.642
<i>Central Standard Time...</i>

09:19.809 --> 09:21.895
<i>our satellites
detected a storm</i>

09:21.978 --> 09:24.481
<i>approaching the</i> Ares 3
<i>mission site on Mars.</i>

09:24.939 --> 09:28.485
<i>At 6:</i><i>45, the storm
had escalated to severe...</i>

09:28.651 --> 09:31.529
<i>and we had no choice
but to abort the mission.</i>

09:31.696 --> 09:34.074
<i>Thanks to the quick action
of Commander Lewis...</i>

09:34.240 --> 09:37.368
<i>astronauts Beck, Johanssen,
Martinez and Vogel...</i>

09:37.535 --> 09:40.246
<i>were all able to reach
the Mars Ascent Vehicle...</i>

09:40.413 --> 09:43.833
<i>and perform an emergency
launch at 7:</i><i>28 Central Time.</i>

09:45.418 --> 09:47.587
Unfortunately,
during the evacuation...

09:47.754 --> 09:51.758
astronaut Mark Watney was
struck by debris and killed.

09:53.009 --> 09:54.886
Commander Lewis
and the rest of her team...

09:55.053 --> 09:57.514
were able to intercept safely
with the <i>Hermes</i>

09:57.597 --> 09:59.682
and are now heading home.

09:59.849 --> 10:02.268
But Mark Watney is dead.

10:02.519 --> 10:03.561
Director Sanders!

10:38.054 --> 10:40.431
<i>Oxygen level critical.</i>

10:46.980 --> 10:49.065
<i>Oxygen level critical.</i>

10:55.905 --> 10:57.574
<i>Oxygen level...</i>

12:15.652 --> 12:17.862
<i>Pressure stable.</i>

15:17.667 --> 15:19.001
Fuck.

15:48.573 --> 15:49.574
Okay.

15:49.949 --> 15:51.033
<i>Okay.</i>

15:53.035 --> 15:57.081
<i>Hello, this is Mark Watney,
astronaut.</i>

15:57.790 --> 16:00.626
<i>I'm entering this log
for the record...</i>

16:01.210 --> 16:03.546
<i>in case I don't make it.</i>

16:06.215 --> 16:10.261
<i>It is 06:</i><i>53 on Sol 19...</i>

16:10.428 --> 16:13.264
<i>and I'm alive.</i>

16:14.599 --> 16:15.725
<i>Obviously.</i>

16:15.892 --> 16:18.436
<i>But I'm guessing that's gonna
come as a surprise</i>

16:18.561 --> 16:21.606
<i>to my crew mates and to NASA.</i>

16:22.064 --> 16:24.734
<i>And to the entire world,
really, so...</i>

16:27.737 --> 16:29.238
<i>Surprise.</i>

16:30.573 --> 16:32.950
<i>I did not die on Sol 18.</i>

16:33.117 --> 16:34.785
<i>Best I can figure...</i>

16:34.952 --> 16:37.330
<i>this length of our primary
communications antenna</i>

16:37.413 --> 16:38.498
<i>broke off...</i>

16:38.664 --> 16:40.833
<i>and tore through
my bio-monitor...</i>

16:41.000 --> 16:43.419
<i>and ripped a hole
in me as well.</i>

16:44.253 --> 16:47.340
<i>But the antenna
and the blood, really,</i>

16:47.423 --> 16:49.467
<i>managed to seal the breach
in my suit...</i>

16:49.634 --> 16:51.093
<i>which kept me alive,</i>

16:51.177 --> 16:53.596
<i>even though the crew
must have thought I was dead.</i>

16:54.430 --> 16:56.766
<i>I have no way to contact NASA.</i>

16:56.933 --> 16:59.936
<i>And even if I could,
it's gonna be four years...</i>

17:00.102 --> 17:02.438
<i>until a manned mission
can reach me.</i>

17:03.439 --> 17:05.691
<i>And I'm in a Hab designed
to last 31 days.</i>

17:05.858 --> 17:08.194
<i>If the oxygen at or breaks,
I'm gonna suffocate.</i>

17:08.361 --> 17:10.655
<i>If the water reclaimer breaks,
I'll die of thirst.</i>

17:10.821 --> 17:13.199
<i>If the Hab breaches,
I'm just gonna, kind of...</i>

17:13.366 --> 17:14.450
<i>implode.</i>

17:15.618 --> 17:18.371
<i>And if by some miracle,
none of that happens...</i>

17:19.121 --> 17:21.249
<i>eventually I'm gonna
run out of food.</i>

17:22.875 --> 17:24.001
<i>So...</i>

17:25.294 --> 17:26.295
<i>yeah.</i>

17:30.466 --> 17:31.551
<i>Yeah.</i>

19:20.841 --> 19:23.093
I'm not gonna die here.

20:24.185 --> 20:25.520
32...

20:26.604 --> 20:27.939
33...

20:29.983 --> 20:31.317
34...

20:32.443 --> 20:33.861
35, 36.

20:34.278 --> 20:37.115
Sweet and sour chicken.

21:09.492 --> 21:10.952
What do we got?

21:14.748 --> 21:16.499
Solanum tuberosum.

21:20.282 --> 21:22.868
<i>Right, let's do the math.</i>

21:23.035 --> 21:26.539
<i>Our surface mission here was
supposed to last 31 sols.</i>

21:26.705 --> 21:28.541
<i>For redundancy, they sent
68 sols worth of food.</i>

21:28.666 --> 21:29.875
<i>That's for 6 people.</i>

21:30.042 --> 21:32.628
<i>So for just me,
that's gonna last 300 sols...</i>

21:32.795 --> 21:35.965
<i>which I figure I can stretch
to 400 if I ration.</i>

21:36.131 --> 21:39.218
<i>So I got to figure out
a way to grow</i>

21:39.301 --> 21:41.053
<i>three years' worth
of food here.</i>

21:41.887 --> 21:43.889
<i>On a planet
where nothing grows.</i>

21:44.890 --> 21:45.891
<i>Luckily...</i>

21:48.686 --> 21:49.728
<i>I'm a botanist.</i>

21:53.816 --> 21:58.571
<i>Mars will come to fear
my botany powers.</i>

22:43.407 --> 22:45.242
<i>Pressure stable.</i>

22:55.628 --> 22:57.296
<i>Staple came out.</i>

23:47.179 --> 23:48.806
Fuck you, Mars.

24:04.029 --> 24:05.531
Johanssen, Jesus.

24:56.039 --> 24:57.916
<i>Sunday, Monday, Happy Days</i>

24:58.000 --> 24:59.501
<i>Tuesday,
Wednesday, Happy Days</i>

25:00.502 --> 25:02.838
<i>Thursday, Friday, Happy Days</i>

25:03.005 --> 25:05.340
<i>The weekend comes,
my cycle hums</i>

25:05.424 --> 25:07.426
<i>Ready to race to you</i>

25:07.593 --> 25:09.928
<i>The problem is water.</i>

25:10.429 --> 25:15.434
I have created
126 square meters of soil.

25:15.601 --> 25:17.603
<i>But every cubic meter
of soil requires</i>

25:17.728 --> 25:20.689
<i>40 liters of water
to be farm able.</i>

25:20.856 --> 25:24.026
<i>So I gotta make
a lot more water.</i>

25:24.902 --> 25:27.029
<i>Good thing is,
I know the recipe.</i>

25:27.613 --> 25:29.615
You take hydrogen,
you add oxygen, you burn.

25:30.115 --> 25:32.785
<i>Now, I have hundreds of liters</i>

25:32.868 --> 25:35.621
<i>of unused hydrazine
at the MDV.</i>

25:35.788 --> 25:38.707
<i>If I run the hydrazine
over an iridium catalyst,</i>

25:38.791 --> 25:41.084
<i>it'll separate into N2 and H2.</i>

25:41.460 --> 25:45.631
<i>And then if I just direct the
hydrogen into a small area...</i>

25:45.964 --> 25:47.800
<i>and burn it.</i>

25:48.133 --> 25:50.552
<i>Luckily,
in the history of humanity...</i>

25:51.261 --> 25:52.971
nothing bad has ever happened

25:53.096 --> 25:55.891
from lighting hydrogen
on fire.

25:56.600 --> 25:58.310
<i>NASA hates fire.</i>

25:58.477 --> 25:59.603
<i>Because of the whole</i>

25:59.728 --> 26:02.481
<i>"fire makes everybody die
in space" thing.</i>

26:02.815 --> 26:04.817
<i>So, everything
they sent us up here with</i>

26:04.900 --> 26:06.318
<i>is flame-retardant...</i>

26:06.485 --> 26:09.613
with the notable
exception of...

26:10.781 --> 26:12.741
Martinez's personal items.

26:13.617 --> 26:14.785
I am sorry, Martinez.

26:14.952 --> 26:16.411
But if you didn't want me to
go through your stuff...

26:16.578 --> 26:19.331
you shouldn't have left me
for dead on a desolate planet.

26:19.957 --> 26:21.416
By the way, I'm figuring
you're gonna be

26:21.500 --> 26:23.752
fine with this,
given my present situation.

26:23.919 --> 26:24.920
<i>What's everybody doing?</i>

26:25.003 --> 26:26.004
<i>Taking a holiday
from being cool?</i>

26:26.171 --> 26:27.756
Counting on you.

26:48.193 --> 26:49.194
Whoo!

27:01.373 --> 27:04.877
So, yeah, I blew myself up.

27:05.043 --> 27:06.712
Best guess...

27:07.379 --> 27:12.634
I forgot to account
for the excess oxygen...

27:13.719 --> 27:17.222
that I've been exhaling
when I did my calculations.

27:17.890 --> 27:20.142
Because I'm stupid.

27:22.728 --> 27:24.813
Yeah, I'm gonna
get back to work here...

27:24.980 --> 27:27.858
just as soon as my ears
stop ringing.

27:32.362 --> 27:33.572
<i>Interesting side note,</i>

27:33.697 --> 27:36.533
<i>this is actually how the Jet
Propulsion Lab was founded.</i>

27:36.700 --> 27:40.162
<i>Five guys at Caltech were
trying to make rocket fuel...</i>

27:40.329 --> 27:41.538
<i>and they nearly burned down
their dorm.</i>

27:41.830 --> 27:43.165
<i>And rather than expel them...</i>

27:43.332 --> 27:46.251
<i>they banished them
to a nearby farm,</i>

27:46.376 --> 27:47.920
<i>told them to keep working.</i>

27:48.754 --> 27:50.589
<i>And now we have
a space program.</i>

27:54.176 --> 27:55.344
Okay.

29:12.587 --> 29:14.297
Hey, there.

29:24.338 --> 29:25.715
<i>The nation
was blessed</i>

29:25.840 --> 29:27.550
<i>to have Mark serving
in our space program.</i>

29:27.967 --> 29:30.219
<i>While his loss
will be deeply felt...</i>

29:30.386 --> 29:33.014
<i>the men and women of NASA
will soldier forth...</i>

29:33.181 --> 29:35.016
<i>onward and upward in the
mission of their agency.</i>

29:36.642 --> 29:38.895
<i>By doing so,
they honor the legacy</i>

29:38.978 --> 29:40.563
<i>Mark's leaving behind...</i>

29:41.147 --> 29:44.066
<i>and they ensure his sacrifice
will not be in vain.</i>

29:55.495 --> 29:56.788
<i>I have the honor of speaking</i>

29:56.913 --> 29:58.539
<i>not only for the men
and women of NASA...</i>

29:59.081 --> 30:00.625
<i>but for people
all over the world...</i>

30:00.833 --> 30:03.044
I thought you gave
a lovely speech, by the way.

30:05.922 --> 30:07.590
I need you to authorize
my satellite time.

30:07.757 --> 30:09.258
It's not gonna happen.

30:09.425 --> 30:11.260
We're funded
for five <i>Ares</i> missions.

30:11.427 --> 30:13.679
I think I can get Congress
to authorize a sixth.

30:13.930 --> 30:14.931
No.

30:15.056 --> 30:16.057
<i>Ares 3</i> evac'd
after 18 sols.

30:16.224 --> 30:18.309
There's half a mission
worth of supplies up there.

30:18.476 --> 30:19.519
I can sell it
at a fraction

30:19.602 --> 30:20.937
of the cost of
a normal mission...

30:21.103 --> 30:23.481
and all I have to know is
what's left of our assets.

30:23.606 --> 30:25.191
You're not the only one
who needs satellite time.

30:25.274 --> 30:27.276
We've got the <i>Ares 4</i> supply
missions coming up.

30:27.360 --> 30:29.612
We should be focusing on
the Schiaparelli Crater.

30:29.695 --> 30:30.696
Okay, we got
12 satellites up there.

30:30.780 --> 30:31.823
Surely we can spare
a few hours...

30:31.906 --> 30:33.991
It's not about
the satellite time, Vince.

30:34.075 --> 30:35.576
We're a public domain
organization.

30:35.660 --> 30:36.702
We need to be
transparent on this.

30:36.786 --> 30:37.829
Okay.

30:37.912 --> 30:39.664
The second we point
the satellites at the Hab...

30:39.747 --> 30:40.790
I broadcast pictures

30:40.873 --> 30:42.667
of Mark Watney's dead body
to the world.

30:42.750 --> 30:44.126
You're afraid of a PR problem?

30:44.377 --> 30:45.962
Of course
I'm afraid of a PR problem.

30:46.128 --> 30:47.338
Another mission?

30:47.505 --> 30:49.841
Congress won't reimburse us
for a paper clip...

30:50.007 --> 30:51.717
if I put a dead astronaut
on the front page

30:51.801 --> 30:52.969
of <i>The Washington Post.</i>

30:53.302 --> 30:55.805
He's not going anywhere,
Teddy. I mean, he's not...

30:56.264 --> 30:58.182
He's not gonna
decompose, you know.

30:58.266 --> 30:59.559
He's gonna be
up there forever.

30:59.725 --> 31:02.520
Meteorology estimates that
he'll be covered in sand...

31:02.687 --> 31:04.397
from normal weather activity
within a year.

31:04.564 --> 31:06.315
We can't wait a year.
We got work to do.

31:06.482 --> 31:08.109
<i>Ares 5</i> won't even launch
for five years.

31:08.192 --> 31:09.318
We have plenty of time.

31:10.069 --> 31:11.070
Okay.

31:12.488 --> 31:13.489
Okay.

31:14.657 --> 31:16.450
Okay, consider this.

31:17.326 --> 31:20.746
Right now,
the world's on our side.

31:20.913 --> 31:22.373
Sympathy
for the Watney family.

31:22.748 --> 31:25.459
<i>Ares 6</i> could
bring his body home.

31:26.043 --> 31:27.920
Now, we don't say that's
the purpose of the mission...

31:28.004 --> 31:29.964
but we make it clear that
that would be a part of it.

31:30.047 --> 31:31.173
We frame it that way.

31:31.757 --> 31:35.136
More support from Congress.
But not if we wait a year.

31:35.887 --> 31:37.889
We wait a year,
nobody gives a shit.

32:10.796 --> 32:12.298
Vincent Kapoor?

32:17.762 --> 32:19.472
6-2.

32:20.348 --> 32:22.016
7-6-2.

32:25.519 --> 32:28.064
Acidalia Planitia.

32:39.075 --> 32:40.117
What?

32:55.257 --> 32:56.425
Hi. Security?

32:56.676 --> 32:58.177
This is Mindy Park in SatCon.

32:58.344 --> 33:00.805
I need the emergency contact
for Vincent Kapoor.

33:01.639 --> 33:02.848
Yes, him.

33:03.557 --> 33:05.226
Yes, it's an emergency!

33:10.481 --> 33:11.482
How sure?

33:12.024 --> 33:13.067
100%.

33:13.693 --> 33:15.277
You've got to be shitting me.

33:16.570 --> 33:18.155
Prove it to me.

33:19.782 --> 33:21.158
For a start...

33:22.451 --> 33:24.245
the solar panels
have been cleaned.

33:24.954 --> 33:26.330
They could have been
cleaned by wind.

33:26.497 --> 33:28.582
Back it up. Look at <i>Rover 2.</i>

33:29.041 --> 33:30.042
According to the logs,

33:30.126 --> 33:31.127
Commander Lewis
took it out on Sol 17...

33:31.293 --> 33:33.379
plugged it into the Hab
to recharge.

33:33.879 --> 33:35.214
It's been moved.

33:35.381 --> 33:37.133
She could have forgotten
to log the move.

33:37.383 --> 33:38.592
No, not likely.

33:38.759 --> 33:39.885
Why don't
we just ask Lewis?

33:40.052 --> 33:41.846
Let's get on CAPCOM and
ask her directly right now.

33:41.929 --> 33:42.888
No. No.

33:42.972 --> 33:44.098
If Watney is really alive,

33:44.181 --> 33:45.808
we don't want
the <i>Ares 3</i> crew to know.

33:46.475 --> 33:47.810
How can you not tell them?

33:47.977 --> 33:50.021
They have another 10 months
on their trip home.

33:50.187 --> 33:51.355
Space travel is dangerous.

33:51.439 --> 33:52.982
They need to be
alert and undistracted.

33:53.149 --> 33:54.358
But they already think
he's dead.

33:54.525 --> 33:55.735
And they'd be devastated
to find out

33:55.818 --> 33:57.445
they left him there alive.

33:57.653 --> 33:59.947
I'm sorry, but you have
not thought this through.

34:00.114 --> 34:03.075
What are we gonna say?
"Dear America...

34:03.284 --> 34:04.952
"remember that astronaut
we killed

34:05.036 --> 34:06.454
"and had a really
nice funeral for?

34:06.620 --> 34:09.123
"Turns out he's alive and we
left him on Mars. Our bad.

34:09.498 --> 34:10.958
"Sincerely, NASA."

34:11.125 --> 34:13.544
Do you realize the shitstorm
that is about to hit us?

34:13.711 --> 34:15.212
How are we going
to handle the public?

34:16.464 --> 34:19.091
Legally, we have 24 hours
to release these pictures.

34:19.258 --> 34:20.426
We release
a statement with them.

34:20.593 --> 34:22.344
We don't want people
working it out on their own.

34:23.179 --> 34:24.263
Yes, sir.

34:24.722 --> 34:26.599
But if my math is right,
he's going to starve to death

34:26.682 --> 34:28.100
long before we can help him.

34:31.103 --> 34:33.439
Can you imagine what
he's going through up there?

34:33.606 --> 34:35.441
He's 50 million miles
away from home.

34:35.524 --> 34:36.817
He thinks he's totally alone.

34:36.984 --> 34:38.444
He thinks we gave up on him.

34:39.320 --> 34:41.947
What does that do to a man,
psychologically?

34:43.407 --> 34:45.451
What the hell
is he thinking right now?

34:49.705 --> 34:52.083
<i>I'm definitely gonna
die up here...</i>

34:52.416 --> 34:56.587
<i>if I have to listen to any
more god-awful disco music.</i>

34:56.921 --> 34:58.380
My God, Commander Lewis,

34:58.464 --> 35:00.382
couldn't you have packed
anything from this century?

35:00.549 --> 35:03.094
<i>Turn the beat around</i>

35:03.260 --> 35:05.346
No, I am not gonna
"turn the beat around."

35:05.429 --> 35:06.889
I refuse to.

35:07.681 --> 35:09.266
Mr. Sanders? Mr. Sanders?

35:11.435 --> 35:12.478
What attempts have been made

35:12.561 --> 35:13.687
to make contact
with Mark Watney?

35:14.522 --> 35:15.898
We're working on it.

35:16.565 --> 35:18.109
Does he have
enough supplies to survive?

35:18.275 --> 35:19.860
We'll be looking into that.

35:20.027 --> 35:21.403
What does this say
about the agency?

35:21.487 --> 35:22.655
Are you gonna resign?

35:22.738 --> 35:23.739
No.

35:23.864 --> 35:25.116
Director Sanders!

35:29.495 --> 35:31.956
<i>It's time to start
thinking long term.</i>

35:32.456 --> 35:34.208
<i>The next NASA mission
is</i> Ares 4...

35:34.750 --> 35:37.503
<i>and it's supposed to land
at Schiaparelli Crater...</i>

35:37.670 --> 35:39.713
<i>3200 kilometers away.</i>

35:40.131 --> 35:43.843
3,200 kilometers.

35:44.426 --> 35:46.929
<i>In four years, when the next</i>
Ares <i>crew arrives,</i>

35:47.012 --> 35:48.389
<i>I'll have to be there.</i>

35:49.431 --> 35:51.308
<i>Which means
I have to get to the crater.</i>

35:53.811 --> 35:55.604
<i>Okay, so here's the rub.</i>

35:55.771 --> 35:57.314
<i>I've got one working Rover</i>

35:57.398 --> 36:01.026
<i>designed to go a max distance
of 35 kilometers...</i>

36:01.193 --> 36:04.071
<i>before the battery has to be
recharged at the Hab.</i>

36:04.864 --> 36:06.198
<i>That's Problem A.</i>

36:06.740 --> 36:10.077
<i>Problem B is this journey's
gonna take me</i>

36:10.161 --> 36:13.581
<i>roughly 50 days to complete.</i>

36:13.747 --> 36:15.833
<i>So I gotta live for 50 days...</i>

36:16.125 --> 36:18.252
<i>inside a Rover
with marginal life support</i>

36:18.335 --> 36:19.587
<i>the size of a small van.</i>

36:22.520 --> 36:26.274
<i>So, in the face of overwhelming
odds, I'm left with only one option.</i>

36:26.858 --> 36:30.070
<i>I'm gonna have to
science the shit out of this.</i>

37:07.440 --> 37:09.359
<i>I almost went down.</i>

37:10.819 --> 37:12.195
<i>Man.</i>

37:27.168 --> 37:29.713
Okay, so, success.

37:31.006 --> 37:32.173
Uh...

37:34.551 --> 37:38.638
I have doubled my battery life
by scavenging <i>Rover 1.</i>

37:39.806 --> 37:42.642
But if I use the heater...

37:43.977 --> 37:48.356
I will burn through half
my battery every day.

37:50.066 --> 37:53.695
If I do not use my heater,
I will be...

37:54.988 --> 37:58.199
slowly killed by
the laws of thermodynamics.

37:58.325 --> 38:03.621
I would love to solve this problem
right now but unfortunately...

38:03.933 --> 38:06.102
<i>my balls are frozen.</i>

38:08.104 --> 38:09.939
I can't. I'm calling it.
I'm calling it.

38:22.452 --> 38:24.370
<i>Good news,
I may have a solution</i>

38:24.454 --> 38:25.913
<i>to my heating problem.</i>

38:26.080 --> 38:28.374
<i>Bad news,
it involves me digging up</i>

38:28.458 --> 38:30.793
<i>the Radioisotope
Thermoelectric Generator.</i>

38:31.294 --> 38:33.463
<i>Now, if I remember
my training correctly,</i>

38:33.546 --> 38:35.340
<i>one of the lessons
was titled...</i>

38:35.506 --> 38:38.718
<i>"Don't Dig Up the Big Box
of Plutonium, Mark."</i>

38:38.885 --> 38:40.803
<i>I get it. RTGs are good
for spacecraft,</i>

38:40.887 --> 38:42.889
<i>but if they rupture
around humans...</i>

38:43.014 --> 38:44.015
<i>no more humans.</i>

38:44.140 --> 38:45.892
<i>Which is why we buried it
when we arrived.</i>

38:46.601 --> 38:47.894
<i>And planted that flag...</i>

38:48.061 --> 38:49.395
<i>so we would never be
stupid enough</i>

38:49.479 --> 38:51.689
<i>to accidentally
go near it again.</i>

38:52.482 --> 38:54.359
<i>But as long as
I don't break it...</i>

38:54.942 --> 38:55.985
<i>I almost just said</i>

38:56.069 --> 38:57.737
<i>"Everything will be fine"
out loud.</i>

38:58.237 --> 39:00.156
Look, the point is,
I'm not cold anymore.

39:00.323 --> 39:01.824
And sure,
I could choose to think

39:01.908 --> 39:03.242
about the fact
that I'm warm...

39:03.409 --> 39:05.078
because I have a decaying

39:05.161 --> 39:07.038
radioactive isotope
riding right behind me...

39:07.205 --> 39:09.874
but right now, I got bigger
problems on my hands.

39:10.041 --> 39:12.418
<i>I have scoured
every single data file</i>

39:12.502 --> 39:14.587
<i>on Commander Lewis'
personal drive.</i>

39:14.754 --> 39:18.966
<i>This is officially
the least disco song she owns.</i>

39:19.967 --> 39:21.969
<i>Lookin' for some hot stuff</i>

39:22.053 --> 39:23.846
<i>Baby, this evenin'</i>

39:23.930 --> 39:26.849
<i>I need some hot stuff,
baby, tonight</i>

39:28.226 --> 39:32.480
<i>I want some hot stuff,
baby, this evenin'</i>

39:32.647 --> 39:37.110
<i>Gotta have some hot stuff
Gotta have some love tonight</i>

39:37.276 --> 39:38.778
<i>Hot stuff</i>

39:40.071 --> 39:42.824
<i>I need hot stuff</i>

39:44.117 --> 39:46.702
<i>I want some hot stuff</i>

39:48.079 --> 39:50.748
<i>I need hot stuff</i>

39:53.835 --> 39:55.420
Yeah.
Where is Watney going?

39:55.586 --> 39:57.839
We believe that
he's preparing for a journey.

39:58.005 --> 40:00.091
He's been conducting
incremental tests...

40:00.258 --> 40:01.676
taking the <i>Rover 2</i>
out for longer

40:01.759 --> 40:03.344
and longer trips each time.

40:05.638 --> 40:06.889
To what end?
Why would he leave

40:06.973 --> 40:08.141
the relative safety
of the Hab?

40:08.570 --> 40:11.406
Well, we think that he plans to
travel to the <i>Ares 4</i> launch site...

40:11.573 --> 40:12.908
in order to make
contact with us,

40:12.991 --> 40:14.660
but it would be
a dangerous gamble.

40:14.827 --> 40:16.036
<i>But if we could talk to him,</i>

40:16.119 --> 40:17.579
<i>we would tell him
to stay put...</i>

40:17.663 --> 40:20.290
<i>and to trust that we are doing
everything in our power</i>

40:20.374 --> 40:22.334
<i>to bring him home alive.</i>

40:22.459 --> 40:24.127
Thank you very much.

40:25.546 --> 40:28.173
You asked me how you did,
and I'm giving you my answer.

40:28.257 --> 40:29.633
My answer is...

40:29.716 --> 40:30.843
Uh...

40:32.386 --> 40:33.679
Look, I'm gonna
make people forget...

40:33.762 --> 40:36.223
there's a very strong possibility
that Mark Watney could die...

40:36.306 --> 40:37.975
because that is what
you are paying me for.

40:38.058 --> 40:39.601
And unfortunately,
I need this job...

40:39.685 --> 40:43.355
because currently I am paying
alimony to two deadbeat ex-husbands...

40:43.438 --> 40:46.191
because somehow, gender equality
has bitten me square in the ass.

40:48.652 --> 40:50.237
Hard to believe.

40:50.320 --> 40:51.363
I left them.

40:54.366 --> 40:55.784
And don't say
"Bring him home alive."

40:55.868 --> 40:58.078
It reminds the world
he might die.

40:58.537 --> 41:00.372
Don't say "Bring him
home alive," Vincent.

41:00.706 --> 41:02.875
You know what? These
interviews aren't easy.

41:03.041 --> 41:06.295
So God forbid I try to say
something proactive and positive.

41:06.378 --> 41:07.379
Annie.

41:07.546 --> 41:09.631
No more Vincent
on TV. Copy that.

41:10.148 --> 41:13.151
76 kilometers.
Am I reading that right?

41:13.568 --> 41:15.070
Are you asking me?

41:15.153 --> 41:16.112
I am.

41:16.196 --> 41:17.280
Yes, sir.

41:17.447 --> 41:20.450
Mark drove two hours
straightaway from the Hab...

41:20.617 --> 41:22.494
did a short EVA and then
drove for another two.

41:22.660 --> 41:25.372
We think the EVA
was to change batteries.

41:25.538 --> 41:26.873
He didn't load up
the oxygen at or

41:26.956 --> 41:28.083
or the water reclaimer?

41:28.458 --> 41:31.127
Every 41 hours,
there's a 17 minute gap.

41:31.294 --> 41:33.213
It's just the way
the orbits work...

41:33.380 --> 41:36.257
so it's possible
that we missed something.

41:36.424 --> 41:38.093
I want that gap
down to four minutes.

41:38.259 --> 41:39.969
I'm giving you
total authority

41:40.053 --> 41:42.263
over satellite trajectories
and orbital adjustments.

41:42.430 --> 41:43.431
Make it happen.

41:43.807 --> 41:44.808
Okay.

41:45.600 --> 41:47.685
Let's assume Miss Park
didn't miss anything.

41:47.852 --> 41:51.272
So Mark's not going
to <i>Ares 4.</i> Yet.

41:51.439 --> 41:52.774
But he's smart enough
to figure out

41:52.857 --> 41:54.776
that's his only chance.

41:54.943 --> 41:57.404
Bruce, what's the earliest we
can get a pre-supply there?

41:57.862 --> 42:00.323
<i>With the positions
of Earth and Mars,</i>

42:00.407 --> 42:01.533
<i>it'll take nine months.</i>

42:03.201 --> 42:05.370
<i>It'll take six months to
build it in the first place.</i>

42:05.537 --> 42:06.579
Three months.

42:07.122 --> 42:08.123
Three?

42:08.206 --> 42:09.416
<i>You're going to say
it's impossible</i>

42:09.499 --> 42:10.917
<i>and then I'm gonna
make a speech...</i>

42:11.084 --> 42:12.210
<i>about the blinding
capabilities</i>

42:12.293 --> 42:13.420
<i>of the JPL team...</i>

42:13.586 --> 42:14.629
<i>and then you're going
to do the math</i>

42:14.712 --> 42:15.755
<i>in your head
and say something like...</i>

42:15.922 --> 42:17.841
"The overtime alone
will be a nightmare."

42:18.007 --> 42:20.051
<i>The overtime alone
will be a nightmare.</i>

42:20.135 --> 42:21.136
Get started.

42:21.302 --> 42:22.679
I'll find you the money.

42:23.888 --> 42:25.432
We need to tell the crew.

42:25.849 --> 42:26.850
Mitch,
we've discussed this.

42:26.933 --> 42:28.184
No, you discussed this.

42:28.601 --> 42:31.729
I'm the one who decides
what's best for the crew.

42:33.148 --> 42:34.399
They deserve to know.

42:35.984 --> 42:37.819
Once there's a real
rescue plan, we'll tell them.

42:37.986 --> 42:39.320
Otherwise, it's moot.

42:39.779 --> 42:42.198
Bruce has three months to get
the payload done.

42:42.365 --> 42:44.159
That's all that
matters right now.

42:44.325 --> 42:45.326
<i>We'll do our best.</i>

42:46.035 --> 42:48.329
Mark dies if you don't.

42:50.123 --> 42:53.626
<i>It's been 48 sols
since I planted the potatoes.</i>

42:53.793 --> 42:55.837
<i>So now it's time
to reap and re-sow.</i>

42:56.004 --> 42:58.548
<i>They grew even better
than I expected.</i>

42:58.715 --> 43:01.551
<i>I now have 400
healthy potato plants.</i>

43:01.801 --> 43:04.971
<i>I dug them up, being careful
to leave their plants alive.</i>

43:05.138 --> 43:06.639
<i>The smaller ones I'll reseed,</i>

43:06.723 --> 43:08.892
<i>the larger ones
are my food supply.</i>

43:09.058 --> 43:12.854
<i>All natural, organic,
Martian-grown potatoes.</i>

43:13.021 --> 43:15.315
<i>You don't hear
that every day, do you?</i>

43:15.982 --> 43:17.650
<i>And by the way,
none of this matters at all...</i>

43:17.817 --> 43:20.987
<i>if I can't figure out a way
to make contact with NASA.</i>

43:42.509 --> 43:43.927
<i>I know what I'm gonna do.</i>

44:12.622 --> 44:13.748
Oh! He's moving again.

44:13.915 --> 44:16.292
Where the hell
is he going?

44:16.459 --> 44:18.628
He hasn't changed course
for 13 days.

44:18.795 --> 44:20.797
He's nowhere near the <i>Ares 4.</i>

44:21.297 --> 44:23.049
Unless he's not taking
a direct route.

44:23.216 --> 44:27.262
He might be trying to avoid
some obstacle.

44:27.428 --> 44:28.638
What obstacle?

44:29.138 --> 44:30.390
It's Acidalia Planitia.

44:30.473 --> 44:32.267
There's nothing out there
except the...

44:34.894 --> 44:36.062
What?

44:39.482 --> 44:41.401
I need a map.

44:47.782 --> 44:48.783
Yeah.

44:49.492 --> 44:50.743
Hey, come on.
What are you doing?

44:50.910 --> 44:51.953
It's all right.
It's all right.

44:52.078 --> 44:53.121
Can I borrow this?

44:53.454 --> 44:54.998
Okay, so where
is the Hab location?

44:55.623 --> 44:58.293
31 .2 degrees north,
28.5 degrees west.

44:58.459 --> 44:59.544
Okay. Mmm-hmm.

44:59.627 --> 45:01.170
Where's Watney?
Uh...

45:01.671 --> 45:02.672
There.

45:02.839 --> 45:03.923
Okay.

45:10.138 --> 45:12.682
Okay. I know where he's going.

45:13.308 --> 45:14.851
I need to get on an airplane.

45:47.550 --> 45:48.676
Vincent, how are you?

45:48.843 --> 45:49.969
Good to see you.

45:50.136 --> 45:51.512
Good flight?
Uh...

45:51.679 --> 45:52.680
Yeah.

45:53.514 --> 45:56.059
It's in storage
just around the corner.

46:30.343 --> 46:31.719
Hey, Vincent,
nice to see you again.

46:31.803 --> 46:32.804
Nice to see you.

46:32.929 --> 46:35.223
What are the chances Mark
can get it working again?

46:35.390 --> 46:38.935
It's hard to say.
We lost contact in '97.

46:39.102 --> 46:41.270
We think
it was battery failure.

46:41.437 --> 46:42.438
Though I'd like to point out

46:42.522 --> 46:45.191
it lasted three times longer
than expected in any...

46:45.358 --> 46:46.651
Nobody's criticizing
JPL's work, Bruce.

46:46.734 --> 46:47.777
I just need to speak
to everybody

46:47.860 --> 46:48.861
that was here in '97.

46:48.945 --> 46:50.697
They're already
here. Guys.

46:50.780 --> 46:52.073
I'd like to introduce
Vincent Kapoor.

46:52.240 --> 46:54.075
Director of Mars Missions
for NASA.

46:54.242 --> 46:55.243
This is our current team...

46:56.035 --> 46:58.246
and our original
project members.

47:14.095 --> 47:15.888
- Is this the replica?
- This is her.

47:16.055 --> 47:17.807
Okay. Let's see it.

47:18.474 --> 47:19.892
<i>Pathfinder.</i>

47:25.398 --> 47:26.899
<i>Pathfinder.</i>

48:37.637 --> 48:39.639
Come on. Come on.

49:00.201 --> 49:02.161
Holy shit. Okay.

49:02.995 --> 49:04.247
"Broadcasting status.

49:04.413 --> 49:05.748
"Listening
for telemetry signal."

49:05.832 --> 49:06.874
Uh-huh.

49:07.834 --> 49:08.835
Okay. Signal acquired.

49:11.212 --> 49:12.338
- All right.
- Okay.

49:12.505 --> 49:13.589
Camera.

49:38.104 --> 49:41.024
Okay, taking
panoramic image now.

49:44.152 --> 49:45.820
You receiving yet?
Yeah.

49:45.903 --> 49:47.655
I just thought we'd all rather
look at a black screen...

49:47.739 --> 49:48.990
instead of a vibrant
red planet.

49:49.073 --> 49:50.074
Excuse me?

49:50.283 --> 49:52.201
Tim's our finest comm tech.

49:52.285 --> 49:54.954
We all appreciate
his acerbic wit.

49:55.330 --> 49:56.497
I will kill you.

50:24.752 --> 50:25.961
Incoming.

50:28.213 --> 50:30.883
"Are you receiving me?"

50:31.884 --> 50:34.052
"Yes. No."

50:35.721 --> 50:37.723
Okay, point
the camera at "yes."

50:38.307 --> 50:40.726
32-minute round-trip
communications time.

50:40.893 --> 50:42.227
All he can do
is ask yes or no questions...

50:42.394 --> 50:44.313
and all we can do
is point the camera.

50:44.480 --> 50:45.898
This won't exactly be
an Algonquin Round Table

50:45.981 --> 50:47.065
of snappy repartee.

50:47.232 --> 50:48.901
Are you kidding me?
Tim, Tim.

50:49.067 --> 50:50.611
- Just point the camera.
- Roger that.

50:51.278 --> 50:53.572
Pointing the camera.

51:04.917 --> 51:05.918
Whoo!

51:08.253 --> 51:09.254
Yes!

51:10.798 --> 51:11.799
Yes!

51:13.258 --> 51:14.927
<i>So here's the rub.</i>

51:15.594 --> 51:17.805
<i>Somehow we have
to have complex</i>

51:17.930 --> 51:19.640
<i>astrophysical engineering
conversations...</i>

51:20.933 --> 51:23.977
<i>Using nothing but
a still-frame camera...</i>

51:24.937 --> 51:26.605
<i>from 1996.</i>

51:29.817 --> 51:31.193
<i>Luckily...</i>

51:32.945 --> 51:34.863
<i>the camera does spin.</i>

51:35.364 --> 51:37.157
So I can make an alphabet.

51:37.699 --> 51:39.368
It can't be our alphabet.

51:39.535 --> 51:41.954
26 characters plus
a question card

51:42.037 --> 51:44.498
into 360 gives us
13 degrees of arc.

51:44.665 --> 51:45.999
That's way too narrow.

51:46.124 --> 51:48.794
I'd never know what the camera
was pointing at.

51:55.467 --> 51:56.635
Ooh.

52:01.223 --> 52:02.391
Hexadecimals.

52:06.895 --> 52:08.480
Hexadecimals to the rescue.

52:08.897 --> 52:10.148
<i>I figured one of you guys</i>

52:10.232 --> 52:12.401
<i>kept an ASCll table
lying around.</i>

52:12.568 --> 52:13.694
<i>And I was right.</i>

52:13.861 --> 52:14.945
<i>Ladies and gentlemen,</i>

52:15.028 --> 52:17.990
<i>I give you super-nerd
Beth Johanssen.</i>

52:18.490 --> 52:21.410
<i>Who also had copies</i>
of Zork ll...

52:21.577 --> 52:24.580
<i>and</i> Leather Goddesses of
Phobos <i>on her personal laptop.</i>

52:25.831 --> 52:27.165
<i>Seriously, Johanssen...</i>

52:27.332 --> 52:30.002
it's like the Smithsonian
of loneliness on there.

52:30.252 --> 52:31.837
<i>Not that I'm complaining.</i>
Yes!

52:32.254 --> 52:34.339
<i>Who am I to talk
about loneliness?</i>

52:54.443 --> 52:56.695
I know where
he's going with this.

53:44.826 --> 53:46.244
"F, O."

53:47.120 --> 53:48.747
"7, W."

54:06.598 --> 54:07.599
Vincent.

54:07.683 --> 54:09.726
Vincent, wake up.

54:21.697 --> 54:24.533
<i>Now that we can have more
complicated conversations...</i>

54:24.700 --> 54:26.994
<i>the smart people at NASA
have sent me instructions</i>

54:27.119 --> 54:28.453
<i>on how to hack the Rover...</i>

54:28.620 --> 54:30.872
<i>so that it can talk
to</i> Pathfinder.

54:31.039 --> 54:32.791
<i>If I hack
a tiny bit of code...</i>

54:32.958 --> 54:35.794
<i>just 20 instructions in the
Rover's operating system...</i>

54:36.378 --> 54:37.963
<i>NASA can link
the Rover to</i> Pathfinder's

54:38.046 --> 54:39.297
<i>broadcasting frequency...</i>

54:40.132 --> 54:42.467
<i>and we're in business.</i>

54:55.814 --> 54:56.815
"Mark...

54:57.232 --> 54:59.526
"this is Vincent Kapoor.

54:59.818 --> 55:03.030
"We've been
watching you since Sol 54.

55:04.031 --> 55:06.491
<i>"The whole world
is rooting for you.</i>

55:07.492 --> 55:10.829
<i>"Amazing job
getting</i> Pathfinder.

55:10.996 --> 55:13.081
<i>"We're working
on rescue plans.</i>

55:13.415 --> 55:16.501
<i>"Meantime, we're putting
together a supply mission...</i>

55:16.668 --> 55:21.339
"to keep you fed
until <i>Ares 4</i> arrives."

55:38.273 --> 55:39.357
Okay.

55:49.284 --> 55:50.285
Okay.

55:51.203 --> 55:53.538
"Glad to hear it. Really
looking forward to not dying."

56:05.884 --> 56:07.302
"How's the crew?"

56:07.469 --> 56:10.138
"What did they say when they
found out I was alive?"

56:31.159 --> 56:33.245
Guys, can we get
some space please?

56:33.870 --> 56:34.830
Me?

56:34.913 --> 56:36.123
Yeah, you.

56:36.289 --> 56:37.499
Give me a sec.

56:43.964 --> 56:45.423
Just tell him.

56:54.182 --> 57:00.147
"We haven't told the crew
you are alive yet.

57:01.857 --> 57:06.153
"We need them to concentrate
on their mission."

57:18.832 --> 57:21.126
What the fuck?

57:21.960 --> 57:23.044
What the fuck?

57:30.343 --> 57:33.054
Okay, he says,
"They don't know I'm alive?

57:33.221 --> 57:34.973
"What the..." F-word.

57:35.140 --> 57:36.391
F-word in gerund form.

57:36.474 --> 57:38.018
F-word, again,
"is wrong with you?"

57:40.645 --> 57:44.149
"Mark, please
watch your language.

57:44.316 --> 57:46.151
"Everything you type

57:46.234 --> 57:50.488
"is being broadcast live
all over the world."

57:53.158 --> 57:54.159
Yeah?

58:02.459 --> 58:03.460
<i>Oh, my God.</i>

58:07.610 --> 58:09.070
What's a fetcher?

58:09.487 --> 58:12.448
Yes, sir. He is under
a tremendous amount of stress.

58:13.867 --> 58:15.118
I understand.
We're working on it.

58:15.285 --> 58:16.744
Thank you,
Mr. President.

58:18.329 --> 58:20.832
I just had to explain to the
President of the United States...

58:20.915 --> 58:23.168
what a beaurocratic
fetcher is.

58:24.419 --> 58:27.005
I made the mistake
of typing it into Google.

58:29.007 --> 58:30.508
Don't.

58:31.676 --> 58:32.886
Problem is,
Mark's right.

58:33.052 --> 58:35.346
The longer we wait,
the worse it's gonna get.

58:35.513 --> 58:36.723
We need to tell the crew.

58:36.890 --> 58:39.517
You're bringing this up while
Vincent is in Pasadena...

58:39.601 --> 58:41.144
so he can't argue
the other side.

58:41.227 --> 58:42.896
I shouldn't have
to answer to Vincent.

58:43.261 --> 58:44.763
Or anyone else.

58:46.890 --> 58:48.475
It's time, Teddy.

59:12.415 --> 59:13.917
<i>Commander Lewis?</i>

59:14.084 --> 59:15.126
Go ahead.

59:15.919 --> 59:17.671
<i>Data dump
is almost complete.</i>

59:18.922 --> 59:20.799
Copy that. On my way.

59:33.770 --> 59:35.021
You're in a hurry.

59:35.188 --> 59:36.356
Yeah.

59:36.523 --> 59:38.441
My son turned
three yesterday.

59:38.608 --> 59:40.485
Should be pictures
of the party.

59:50.954 --> 59:52.872
Got a batch
of personals.

59:52.956 --> 59:55.125
Dispatching them
to your laptops now.

59:56.292 --> 59:59.462
I don't need to read Vogel's
weird German fetish emails.

59:59.629 --> 01:00:01.339
They're
telemetry updates.

01:00:01.631 --> 01:00:02.882
Hey, whatever
does it for you, man.

01:00:04.467 --> 01:00:05.635
There's a video message.

01:00:05.802 --> 01:00:07.470
It's addressed
to the whole crew.

01:00:08.138 --> 01:00:10.223
Play it. Come on, guys.

01:00:10.640 --> 01:00:12.475
I wanna get in on
that telemetry action.

01:00:16.396 --> 01:00:19.190
<i>Hi, hello. It's Mitch.
Mitch Henderson.</i>

01:00:19.649 --> 01:00:21.484
<i>I have some news.</i>

01:00:21.651 --> 01:00:24.320
<i>There's no subtle way
to put this.</i>

01:00:25.071 --> 01:00:26.990
<i>Mark Watney is still alive.</i>

01:00:27.157 --> 01:00:28.491
Oh, my God.

01:00:29.159 --> 01:00:31.286
<i>I know that's a surprise
and I know</i>

01:00:31.369 --> 01:00:32.829
<i>you will have
a lot of questions.</i>

01:00:32.996 --> 01:00:35.707
<i>But here are the basics.</i>

01:00:35.874 --> 01:00:38.334
<i>He's alive and he's healthy.</i>

01:00:39.878 --> 01:00:43.673
<i>We found out two months ago
and decided not to tell you.</i>

01:00:44.007 --> 01:00:45.175
Two months?
Oh, my God.

01:00:45.341 --> 01:00:47.510
<i>I was strongly
against that decision.</i>

01:00:48.178 --> 01:00:50.930
<i>We're telling you
now because...</i>

01:00:51.097 --> 01:00:53.433
<i>we finally have
communication with him...</i>

01:00:53.933 --> 01:00:56.352
<i>and a viable rescue plan.</i>

01:00:56.811 --> 01:00:59.105
<i>We'll give you a write-up
of what happened,</i>

01:00:59.189 --> 01:01:00.523
<i>a full write-up of everything.</i>

01:01:00.690 --> 01:01:02.192
<i>But it's not your fault.</i>

01:01:02.358 --> 01:01:04.986
<i>Mark stresses that
every time it comes up.</i>

01:01:06.070 --> 01:01:08.531
<i>So... Yeah...</i>

01:01:10.492 --> 01:01:12.494
<i>Take some time to absorb this.</i>

01:01:13.036 --> 01:01:15.747
<i>And send all
the questions you want.</i>

01:01:15.830 --> 01:01:17.290
<i>We'll answer them.</i>

01:01:18.625 --> 01:01:20.251
<i>All right. Henderson out.</i>

01:01:20.627 --> 01:01:22.420
Holy shit, he's alive.

01:01:23.379 --> 01:01:24.547
I left him behind.

01:01:24.714 --> 01:01:25.715
- No.
- No.

01:01:25.882 --> 01:01:27.467
We all left together.

01:01:27.634 --> 01:01:29.385
You were following orders.

01:01:33.056 --> 01:01:35.058
I left him behind.

01:01:44.734 --> 01:01:45.777
<i>So now that
NASA can talk to me,</i>

01:01:45.902 --> 01:01:46.945
<i>they won't shut up.</i>

01:01:47.487 --> 01:01:50.281
<i>They want constant updates
on every Hab system...</i>

01:01:50.448 --> 01:01:52.033
<i>and they got
a room full of people</i>

01:01:52.116 --> 01:01:54.077
<i>trying to micromanage
my crops.</i>

01:01:54.244 --> 01:01:55.745
<i>Which is awesome.</i>

01:01:56.621 --> 01:01:58.623
<i>Look, I don't mean to sound
arrogant or anything...</i>

01:01:58.790 --> 01:02:03.503
<i>but I am the greatest botanist
on this planet, so...</i>

01:02:05.004 --> 01:02:06.214
<i>One big bonus</i>

01:02:06.297 --> 01:02:08.091
<i>to this communication
with NASA again...</i>

01:02:08.258 --> 01:02:09.676
<i>is the email.
I'm getting them again.</i>

01:02:09.843 --> 01:02:12.178
<i>Big data dumps
like when I was on the</i> Hermes.

01:02:12.345 --> 01:02:13.805
<i>I even got one
from the president.</i>

01:02:14.347 --> 01:02:16.933
<i>The coolest one, though,
the coolest one I got...</i>

01:02:17.100 --> 01:02:18.268
<i>was from
University of Chicago,</i>

01:02:18.351 --> 01:02:19.853
<i>my Alma mater.</i>

01:02:20.019 --> 01:02:22.522
They say that once you
grow crops somewhere...

01:02:22.689 --> 01:02:24.649
you've officially
colonized it.

01:02:25.525 --> 01:02:27.777
<i>So, technically...</i>

01:02:28.820 --> 01:02:30.196
<i>I colonized Mars.</i>

01:02:35.118 --> 01:02:37.120
In your face, Neil Armstrong.

01:02:37.996 --> 01:02:39.789
In other news,
there's been a request...

01:02:39.956 --> 01:02:41.624
for me to pose
for a photograph

01:02:41.708 --> 01:02:43.793
on my next transmission.

01:02:44.627 --> 01:02:45.920
I'm trying
to figure out whether

01:02:46.004 --> 01:02:48.798
I should go with
"High School Senior"...

01:02:48.965 --> 01:02:51.968
or "coquettish ingénue."

01:02:54.053 --> 01:02:55.138
But I'm not really sure

01:02:55.221 --> 01:02:56.806
how that's gonna look
with my spacesuit on.

01:03:23.166 --> 01:03:24.167
Hey!

01:03:26.669 --> 01:03:28.171
What is he doing?

01:03:28.338 --> 01:03:30.423
I asked for a photo, and what,
he's The Fonz?

01:03:30.590 --> 01:03:32.258
<i>Just be grateful
we got you something, Annie.</i>

01:03:32.425 --> 01:03:34.385
I can't use this, Vincent,
and you know that.

01:03:34.552 --> 01:03:35.845
I need a picture of his face.

01:03:36.012 --> 01:03:37.555
<i>I could tell him
to take off his helmet...</i>

01:03:37.722 --> 01:03:39.432
but then he'd, you know,
die, so...

01:03:40.934 --> 01:03:41.976
<i>We'll release
the photo</i>

01:03:42.060 --> 01:03:43.353
<i>when we detail
the rescue operation.</i>

01:03:43.519 --> 01:03:45.146
I want to announce
we're launching

01:03:45.229 --> 01:03:46.439
some supplies to him
next year...

01:03:46.606 --> 01:03:48.107
during the Hohmann
Transfer Window.

01:03:48.691 --> 01:03:50.944
Bruce, your team's still
on schedule?

01:03:51.110 --> 01:03:53.029
<i>It'll be tight,
but we'll make it.</i>

01:03:53.196 --> 01:03:54.697
Nine-month travel time.

01:03:54.781 --> 01:03:57.283
That puts the probe
to Mars on Sol 868.

01:03:57.450 --> 01:03:59.202
Did we get
the botany team's analysis?

01:03:59.369 --> 01:04:02.705
<i>Yeah, they estimate Mark's
crops will last till Sol 912.</i>

01:04:02.872 --> 01:04:05.625
<i>They grudgingly admit that
he's doing great work.</i>

01:04:05.708 --> 01:04:06.709
Grudgingly?

01:04:06.793 --> 01:04:07.794
<i>Yeah. Mark has a tendency
to tell them</i>

01:04:07.877 --> 01:04:08.920
<i>to have sex with themselves...</i>

01:04:09.045 --> 01:04:10.755
<i>whenever they question
one of his decisions.</i>

01:04:11.089 --> 01:04:12.173
Get him
in line, Vincent.

01:04:12.256 --> 01:04:14.300
We can't afford
any miscommunication.

01:04:14.384 --> 01:04:15.593
I hate this margin.

01:04:15.677 --> 01:04:19.347
912 Sols worth of food.
We get there on 868.

01:04:20.306 --> 01:04:22.725
And that's assuming
nothing goes wrong.

01:05:07.937 --> 01:05:10.606
<i>Suit breach detected.</i>

01:05:18.364 --> 01:05:20.867
<i>Oxygen level critical.</i>

01:05:34.380 --> 01:05:37.133
<i>Oxygen level, 10%.</i>

01:05:51.773 --> 01:05:54.317
<i>Oxygen level, 5%.</i>

01:06:03.826 --> 01:06:05.661
<i>Suit pressure, stable.</i>

01:08:01.726 --> 01:08:03.644
<i>Pressure stable.</i>

01:08:37.178 --> 01:08:40.181
God! God, God, God, God!

01:08:59.116 --> 01:09:00.868
Crops are dead.

01:09:01.035 --> 01:09:03.371
Complete loss of pressure
boiled off most of the water.

01:09:03.537 --> 01:09:04.789
Any bacteria that survived,

01:09:04.872 --> 01:09:06.457
died in the subzero
temperatures...

01:09:06.624 --> 01:09:08.542
when exposed to
Mars' atmosphere.

01:09:08.709 --> 01:09:10.169
How long does he have?

01:09:11.671 --> 01:09:13.297
He can still eat
the potatoes he has,

01:09:13.381 --> 01:09:15.216
he just can't grow any more...

01:09:15.841 --> 01:09:18.386
so give him 200 sols.

01:09:18.636 --> 01:09:21.722
Rations get him to what?
Sol 409?

01:09:21.847 --> 01:09:24.517
Mmm-hmm.
So with potatoes, 609.

01:09:24.684 --> 01:09:27.311
So by Sol 868,
he'll be long dead.

01:09:27.478 --> 01:09:29.355
We're gonna have to launch
as soon as possible...

01:09:29.522 --> 01:09:30.856
which changes our travel time.

01:09:32.358 --> 01:09:33.818
Yeah, we're working on it.

01:09:34.860 --> 01:09:38.864
Prelim estimates call
for a 414-day trip.

01:09:39.490 --> 01:09:41.575
It's Sol 135 now.

01:09:41.742 --> 01:09:43.744
We need 13 days
to mount the boosters,

01:09:43.869 --> 01:09:45.079
perform inspections...

01:09:45.246 --> 01:09:47.873
which gives
Bruce and his team...

01:09:48.749 --> 01:09:51.002
47 days to make this probe.

01:09:52.336 --> 01:09:55.548
I'll let you call Bruce,
give him the news.

01:09:58.592 --> 01:09:59.885
Okay.

01:10:03.097 --> 01:10:05.766
I'm gonna need
a change of clothes.

01:10:14.191 --> 01:10:15.901
<i>"Dear Mark...</i>

01:10:16.444 --> 01:10:21.073
<i>"Apparently NASA's letting us
talk to you now.</i>

01:10:22.408 --> 01:10:25.870
"And I drew the short straw.

01:10:29.707 --> 01:10:33.294
<i>"Sorry we left you
behind on Mars.</i>

01:10:33.461 --> 01:10:35.921
"But we just don't like you.

01:10:38.424 --> 01:10:43.637
"Also, it's a lot roomier
on the <i>Hermes</i> without you.

01:10:44.305 --> 01:10:47.641
<i>"We have to take turns
doing your tasks.</i>

01:10:48.267 --> 01:10:51.562
"But, I mean,
it's only botany.

01:10:53.147 --> 01:10:54.398
"It's not real science."

01:10:54.565 --> 01:10:55.608
Oh, Jesus.

01:10:57.234 --> 01:10:58.235
<i>"How's Mars?"</i>

01:11:03.282 --> 01:11:06.327
<i>"Dear Martinez,
Mars is fine.</i>

01:11:07.953 --> 01:11:10.247
<i>"I accidentally
blew up the Hab...</i>

01:11:10.915 --> 01:11:12.583
<i>"but unfortunately,</i>

01:11:12.666 --> 01:11:17.463
<i>"all of Commander Lewis'
disco music still survived."</i>

01:11:18.172 --> 01:11:19.173
"But unfortunately,

01:11:19.298 --> 01:11:22.093
"all of Commander Lewis'
disco music still survived."

01:11:22.968 --> 01:11:25.179
<i>"Every day,
I go outside</i>

01:11:25.262 --> 01:11:27.807
<i>"and look at
the vast horizons."</i>

01:11:28.182 --> 01:11:29.600
"Every day,
I go outside

01:11:29.683 --> 01:11:32.144
"and look at
the vast horizons."

01:11:32.311 --> 01:11:33.646
<i>"Just because I can."</i>

01:11:34.021 --> 01:11:36.023
"Just because I can.

01:11:36.857 --> 01:11:39.693
"Tell the others
I said hello."

01:11:42.363 --> 01:11:43.781
"Will do, buddy."

01:13:13.815 --> 01:13:15.525
<i>Pressure stable.</i>

01:14:27.462 --> 01:14:29.547
5, 10...

01:14:30.256 --> 01:14:33.051
15, 20, 30, 35...

01:14:34.552 --> 01:14:35.804
40.

01:14:36.471 --> 01:14:37.472
52.

01:15:06.459 --> 01:15:07.627
Rich.

01:15:08.795 --> 01:15:09.796
Rich.

01:15:09.963 --> 01:15:11.005
Wake up.

01:15:13.842 --> 01:15:16.010
Sorry, but they're asking for
the probe courses.

01:15:16.177 --> 01:15:17.679
What time is it?

01:15:17.846 --> 01:15:19.681
It's 3:42.

01:15:25.103 --> 01:15:27.522
Look, I know we're
coming at this backwards...

01:15:27.605 --> 01:15:29.357
but we can't commit
to a firm launch date

01:15:29.440 --> 01:15:30.942
with this many unknowns.

01:15:31.985 --> 01:15:33.194
It's all right. Um...

01:15:33.862 --> 01:15:36.656
All 25 models for launch will
take 414 days to reach Mars.

01:15:36.823 --> 01:15:38.366
They vary slightly
in thrust duration...

01:15:38.533 --> 01:15:40.326
and the fuel requirement
is nearly identical.

01:15:41.703 --> 01:15:43.538
Not a good time
to launch, is it?

01:15:43.705 --> 01:15:46.541
Yeah, Earth and Mars
are really badly positioned.

01:15:46.708 --> 01:15:48.710
Heck, it'd almost
be easier if you...

01:15:51.546 --> 01:15:52.714
Almost easier to what?

01:15:53.965 --> 01:15:55.341
I need more coffee.

01:15:55.800 --> 01:15:56.801
Ahh!

01:15:57.385 --> 01:15:58.386
Are you all right?

01:15:58.553 --> 01:15:59.554
It's fine.

01:16:03.892 --> 01:16:05.560
Almost easier to what?

01:16:17.198 --> 01:16:19.242
You do understand
I'm your boss, right?

01:16:20.284 --> 01:16:21.285
Uh-huh.

01:16:23.329 --> 01:16:25.581
Okay, let's ask
the $200 million...

01:16:25.665 --> 01:16:27.041
I'm sorry 500...

01:16:27.124 --> 01:16:29.210
Is that a 5?
<i>At least.</i>

01:16:29.877 --> 01:16:33.381
All right, let's ask
the very, very expensive question.

01:16:34.131 --> 01:16:35.716
Is the probe going
to be ready on time?

01:16:35.883 --> 01:16:37.551
<i>We're behind.</i>
Give me a number.

01:16:37.885 --> 01:16:40.930
<i>15 days. 15 days
and I can get it done.</i>

01:16:41.305 --> 01:16:42.515
All right, let's
create 15 days.

01:16:42.598 --> 01:16:44.183
13 days to mount the probe.

01:16:44.350 --> 01:16:45.518
Can we reduce?

01:16:45.685 --> 01:16:47.937
It actually only takes three
days to mount the probe.

01:16:48.104 --> 01:16:49.855
And we can get that down
to two, right?

01:16:50.022 --> 01:16:51.357
<i>I can get it down to two.</i>

01:16:51.607 --> 01:16:53.192
10 days are for tests
and inspections.

01:16:53.359 --> 01:16:55.861
How often do those inspections
reveal a problem?

01:16:57.196 --> 01:16:58.781
Are you suggesting
we don't do the inspections?

01:16:58.948 --> 01:17:01.200
Right now I'm asking how often
they reveal a problem.

01:17:01.367 --> 01:17:02.368
One in 20.

01:17:02.535 --> 01:17:04.120
But that's grounds
for countdown halt.

01:17:04.287 --> 01:17:05.371
We can't take
that chance.

01:17:05.538 --> 01:17:07.873
Anyone else know a safer way
to buy more time?

01:17:12.295 --> 01:17:14.630
Dr. Keller, stretch Watney's
rations four more days.

01:17:14.797 --> 01:17:16.549
You're not gonna like it,
but that'll get us to 15.

01:17:16.674 --> 01:17:17.633
And we'll cancel
the inspections.

01:17:17.717 --> 01:17:18.801
Teddy.

01:17:18.884 --> 01:17:20.636
Sir, if that ever got out...
It's on me.

01:17:20.970 --> 01:17:22.138
You got your two weeks.

01:17:22.305 --> 01:17:23.514
Get it done.

01:17:24.711 --> 01:17:26.588
So,
now I have to hold out

01:17:26.671 --> 01:17:28.715
until the probe gets here
with more food.

01:17:28.798 --> 01:17:31.593
You want to see what minimal
calorie count looks like?

01:17:31.760 --> 01:17:33.303
Standard issue ration.

01:17:33.470 --> 01:17:37.098
But instead of three of
these every one day...

01:17:37.557 --> 01:17:41.144
I'm now eating one of these
every three days.

01:17:41.561 --> 01:17:42.729
This is meatloaf.

01:17:42.812 --> 01:17:44.272
So, here's today's
allotment...

01:17:44.355 --> 01:17:46.983
<i>which, of course, I've
supplemented with a potato...</i>

01:17:47.317 --> 01:17:49.736
<i>which I'm beginning to hate
with the fiery passion...</i>

01:17:49.819 --> 01:17:51.446
of 1,000 suns.

01:17:52.197 --> 01:17:53.990
And now, they've asked me...

01:17:54.991 --> 01:17:56.242
to do that.

01:18:00.997 --> 01:18:02.248
The point is...

01:18:03.666 --> 01:18:06.669
<i>"stretch the rations
four more days"...</i>

01:18:07.712 --> 01:18:09.547
<i>is a real dick-punch.</i>

01:18:13.343 --> 01:18:16.346
<i>I'm gonna dip this potato
in some crushed Vicodin.</i>

01:18:18.348 --> 01:18:20.100
And there's nobody
who can stop me.

01:18:30.235 --> 01:18:33.530
<i>It has been seven days
since I ran out of ketchup.</i>

01:18:36.157 --> 01:18:37.826
And since we've
pushed launch...

01:18:37.909 --> 01:18:39.953
we need to account for the
new satellite adjustments.

01:18:40.662 --> 01:18:42.539
We still haven't received
the old satellite adjustments.

01:18:43.123 --> 01:18:44.749
Well, I asked for those
two weeks ago.

01:18:44.833 --> 01:18:45.834
What's Rich doing?

01:18:46.209 --> 01:18:47.669
We're scared to go in there.

01:18:55.885 --> 01:18:57.595
Rich, what the hell
are you doing?

01:18:58.638 --> 01:19:00.348
I need you to get me
some supercomputer time.

01:19:04.727 --> 01:19:06.229
When did you last sleep?

01:19:07.981 --> 01:19:09.274
It's important, Mike.

01:19:12.652 --> 01:19:14.279
All right,
I'll do it.

01:19:14.904 --> 01:19:15.905
Good.

01:19:25.415 --> 01:19:27.625
Up. Up, up. Yeah.

01:19:28.084 --> 01:19:29.794
Let's go up here.

01:19:29.919 --> 01:19:30.837
Labels facing up.

01:19:36.661 --> 01:19:37.787
This is the flight director.

01:19:37.954 --> 01:19:39.581
Begin launch status check.

01:19:39.747 --> 01:19:42.333
Roger that, Flight.
Beginning launch status check.

01:19:42.500 --> 01:19:44.460
Do you believe
in God, Vincent?

01:19:45.795 --> 01:19:46.796
Yeah.

01:19:47.797 --> 01:19:49.507
Yeah, my father
was a Hindu,

01:19:49.591 --> 01:19:51.926
my mother's a Baptist,
so, yeah...

01:19:52.093 --> 01:19:53.678
I believe in several.

01:19:56.347 --> 01:19:58.182
We'll take all
the help we can get.

01:19:58.349 --> 01:20:00.018
<i>Launch status check complete.</i>

01:20:00.351 --> 01:20:01.686
This is Flight.

01:20:01.853 --> 01:20:03.313
We are go for launch.

01:20:03.521 --> 01:20:04.522
<i>Proceeding with the count.</i>

01:20:04.689 --> 01:20:06.190
<i>10... 9...</i>

01:20:06.357 --> 01:20:07.483
<i>8...</i>

01:20:07.650 --> 01:20:08.860
<i>7... 6...</i>

01:20:09.027 --> 01:20:10.528
<i>Main engines start.</i>

01:20:10.695 --> 01:20:11.988
<i>4... 3...</i>

01:20:12.488 --> 01:20:13.489
<i>2...</i>

01:20:13.656 --> 01:20:14.657
<i>1.</i>

01:20:15.950 --> 01:20:17.619
<i>Liftoff.</i>

01:20:19.621 --> 01:20:20.997
<i>Good thrust.</i>

01:20:25.001 --> 01:20:26.377
<i>Performance is nominal.</i>

01:20:40.183 --> 01:20:42.352
<i>The tail is giving good data.</i>

01:20:43.853 --> 01:20:45.480
<i>She's rock
solid at this point, Flight.</i>

01:20:45.563 --> 01:20:46.564
<i>TVCs look good.</i>

01:20:58.159 --> 01:20:59.577
We're getting
a little shimmy, Flight.

01:21:00.662 --> 01:21:01.913
Say again?

01:21:02.372 --> 01:21:04.415
We are getting
a very large precession.

01:21:04.874 --> 01:21:05.875
Are we good?

01:21:07.168 --> 01:21:08.544
<i>Flight,
it's hitting the red line.</i>

01:21:08.711 --> 01:21:11.214
It's spinning on the long axis
around a 17-degree precession.

01:21:12.757 --> 01:21:14.258
Launch, what's happening?

01:21:14.425 --> 01:21:16.094
Force on Iris is 7 G's.

01:21:16.386 --> 01:21:18.096
We've lost
readings on the probe, Flight.

01:21:21.933 --> 01:21:24.769
Oh, Jesus Christ.

01:21:28.189 --> 01:21:29.440
We've lost it, Flight.

01:21:33.361 --> 01:21:34.362
SatCon?

01:21:34.529 --> 01:21:35.613
<i>No satellite acquisition</i>

01:21:35.738 --> 01:21:36.781
<i>of signal.</i>

01:21:40.159 --> 01:21:41.160
<i>L.O.S.</i>

01:21:41.244 --> 01:21:42.286
L.O.S. here, too.

01:21:42.787 --> 01:21:43.955
<i>U.S. Destroyer</i> Stockton

01:21:44.038 --> 01:21:46.082
<i>reporting debris
falling from the sky.</i>

01:21:46.207 --> 01:21:47.291
<i>Everyone in the ICC,</i>

01:21:47.417 --> 01:21:48.876
<i>maintain your positions
at your consoles.</i>

01:21:49.043 --> 01:21:51.129
GC, lock the doors.

01:22:19.657 --> 01:22:21.451
<i>"Commander Lewis...</i>

01:22:21.617 --> 01:22:25.246
<i>"I may need you
to do something for me.</i>

01:22:26.831 --> 01:22:31.669
<i>"If I die, I need you to
check in on my parents.</i>

01:22:32.837 --> 01:22:36.257
<i>"They'll wanna hear all about
our time here on Mars.</i>

01:22:37.800 --> 01:22:39.802
<i>"I know that sucks.</i>

01:22:39.969 --> 01:22:43.181
<i>"And it'll be hard
talking to a couple...</i>

01:22:44.098 --> 01:22:46.476
<i>"about their dead son.</i>

01:22:47.518 --> 01:22:49.270
"It's a lot to ask.

01:22:51.689 --> 01:22:53.858
"Which is why I'm asking you.

01:22:54.942 --> 01:22:56.861
<i>"I'm not giving up.</i>

01:22:57.028 --> 01:23:02.033
<i>"We just need to prepare
for every outcome.</i>

01:23:03.701 --> 01:23:05.661
"Please tell them...

01:23:07.705 --> 01:23:09.540
"Tell them I love what I do...

01:23:13.961 --> 01:23:15.296
"and I'm really good at it.

01:23:17.965 --> 01:23:21.010
<i>"And that I'm dying...</i>

01:23:22.303 --> 01:23:24.806
"for something big...

01:23:26.349 --> 01:23:28.392
"and beautiful...

01:23:32.355 --> 01:23:34.398
"and greater than me.

01:23:36.234 --> 01:23:38.861
<i>"Tell them
I said I can live with that.</i>

01:23:39.904 --> 01:23:41.572
<i>"And tell them...</i>

01:23:42.740 --> 01:23:46.369
<i>"thank you for being
my mom and dad."</i>

01:23:49.997 --> 01:23:50.998
<i>We substituted
protein cubes</i>

01:23:51.082 --> 01:23:52.166
<i>for the standard rations.</i>

01:23:52.333 --> 01:23:54.085
<i>The thrust
of the launch combined</i>

01:23:54.168 --> 01:23:55.878
<i>with the simultaneous
lateral vibration...</i>

01:23:56.045 --> 01:24:00.842
<i>liquefied the cubes and
created an unbalanced load.</i>

01:24:00.925 --> 01:24:02.260
<i>Vincent, why wasn't
this addressed</i>

01:24:02.385 --> 01:24:03.928
<i>in the inspections phase?</i>

01:24:04.095 --> 01:24:05.596
<i>In order to make
our launch window,</i>

01:24:05.721 --> 01:24:07.682
<i>we were forced
to accelerate our schedule.</i>

01:24:09.600 --> 01:24:11.686
Their astronaut
is going to die.

01:24:13.563 --> 01:24:15.565
Of course there
are other ways...

01:24:17.692 --> 01:24:20.444
...the Tat yang Shen booster. Our
engineers have run the numbers,

01:24:20.695 --> 01:24:23.447
and it has enough fuel
for a Mars injection orbit.

01:24:24.615 --> 01:24:25.950
Why hasn't NASA
approached us?

01:24:26.367 --> 01:24:27.451
They don't know.

01:24:27.952 --> 01:24:30.413
Our booster technology
is classified.

01:24:33.040 --> 01:24:35.960
So if we do nothing...

01:24:36.586 --> 01:24:40.631
The world would never know
we could have helped.

01:24:41.716 --> 01:24:46.554
Then, for the sake of argument,
let's say we decide to help them...

01:24:47.221 --> 01:24:51.142
We'd be giving up a booster and
effectively cancelling Tat yang Shen.

01:24:52.226 --> 01:24:57.315
We need to keep this among scientists,
a co-operation between space agencies.

01:25:00.151 --> 01:25:01.152
Yes.

01:25:02.653 --> 01:25:04.405
Yes, I understand.

01:25:07.575 --> 01:25:08.784
Thank you.

01:25:12.413 --> 01:25:13.497
Yes!

01:25:14.832 --> 01:25:15.833
<i>All right.</i>

01:25:15.958 --> 01:25:17.585
Thanks to my uncle Tommy
in China,

01:25:17.668 --> 01:25:19.503
we get another chance at this.

01:25:19.670 --> 01:25:22.757
Now, we finished
the <i>Iris</i> probe in 62 days.

01:25:22.924 --> 01:25:26.302
We are now gonna attempt
to finish it in 28.

01:25:51.994 --> 01:25:53.287
We can jettison
any kind of landing system.

01:25:53.371 --> 01:25:54.789
We're only sending rations.

01:25:54.956 --> 01:25:56.624
We can crash-land on Mars.

01:25:58.000 --> 01:25:59.335
You should hang up
the phone.

01:25:59.669 --> 01:26:01.629
I'm sorry, who are you?
My name is Rich Purnell.

01:26:01.796 --> 01:26:02.797
I work in Astrodynamics,

01:26:02.880 --> 01:26:04.048
and you should hang up
the phone right now.

01:26:06.300 --> 01:26:07.343
All right, all right.

01:26:08.135 --> 01:26:09.512
I'm gonna call you back.

01:26:10.846 --> 01:26:12.306
I know how to
save Mark Watney.

01:26:13.557 --> 01:26:15.893
Your probe plan won't work.
Too many things can go wrong.

01:26:16.227 --> 01:26:17.228
I've got a better way.

01:26:17.395 --> 01:26:18.479
Astrodynamics?

01:26:18.646 --> 01:26:19.981
Yeah.

01:26:20.410 --> 01:26:22.954
What the hell
is "Project Elrond"?

01:26:23.372 --> 01:26:24.373
I had to
make something up.

01:26:24.539 --> 01:26:25.874
But "Elrond"?

01:26:26.375 --> 01:26:28.377
Because it's a secret meeting.

01:26:28.585 --> 01:26:29.628
How do you know that?

01:26:29.711 --> 01:26:31.129
Why does "Elrond" mean
"secret meeting"?

01:26:31.296 --> 01:26:33.882
<i>The Council of Elrond.
It's from</i> Lord of the Rings.

01:26:34.049 --> 01:26:35.092
<i>Lord of the Rings.</i>

01:26:35.175 --> 01:26:37.386
It's the meeting where they
decide to destroy the One Ring.

01:26:37.469 --> 01:26:39.137
<i>if we're gonna call
something "Project Elrond"...</i>

01:26:39.304 --> 01:26:41.473
I would like my code name
to be "Glorfindel."

01:26:41.640 --> 01:26:43.600
Okay, I hate
every one of you.

01:26:43.725 --> 01:26:46.144
What, Teddy doesn't even
know about this yet?

01:26:46.353 --> 01:26:47.479
I'm sorry,
who are you?

01:26:47.646 --> 01:26:49.356
This is Rich Purnell,
Astrodynamics.

01:26:50.482 --> 01:26:51.900
Tell them what
you just told me.

01:26:52.234 --> 01:26:55.237
I can get the <i>Hermes</i>
back to Mars by Sol 561.

01:26:56.988 --> 01:26:57.989
How?

01:27:02.119 --> 01:27:04.788
Could you stand right
there for me, please?

01:27:06.498 --> 01:27:07.499
Thanks.

01:27:07.666 --> 01:27:08.750
Right there. Great.

01:27:08.917 --> 01:27:13.088
And could you stand
right there? Right there.

01:27:17.801 --> 01:27:21.596
Okay, let's pretend that
this stapler is the <i>Hermes...</i>

01:27:21.763 --> 01:27:24.015
and you are...

01:27:24.182 --> 01:27:25.308
I'm sorry,
what's your name again?

01:27:25.559 --> 01:27:28.937
Teddy. I'm the Director
of NASA.

01:27:29.271 --> 01:27:31.231
Cool. Teddy,
you're Earth.

01:27:31.565 --> 01:27:33.775
And right now, the <i>Hermes</i>
is headed towards you...

01:27:33.942 --> 01:27:36.445
starting its month-long
deceleration to intercept.

01:27:36.611 --> 01:27:39.114
But instead,
what I'm proposing is...

01:27:43.118 --> 01:27:45.370
We start accelerating
immediately

01:27:45.454 --> 01:27:47.122
to preserve velocity
and gain even more.

01:27:47.289 --> 01:27:48.498
We don't intercept
with Earth at all...

01:27:48.582 --> 01:27:49.916
but we come close enough

01:27:50.000 --> 01:27:52.127
to get a gravity assist
and adjust course.

01:27:52.294 --> 01:27:54.171
While we're doing that...

01:27:56.381 --> 01:27:58.008
we resupply with the probe...

01:27:58.133 --> 01:27:59.217
The <i>Tat yang Shen.</i>

01:27:59.384 --> 01:28:01.011
...pick up whatever
provisions we need...

01:28:01.094 --> 01:28:03.263
and now we're accelerating
towards Mars.

01:28:05.807 --> 01:28:07.184
You're Mars.

01:28:07.392 --> 01:28:10.312
Now, we're going too fast
at this point to fall into orbit...

01:28:10.479 --> 01:28:11.813
but we can do a flyby.

01:28:11.980 --> 01:28:14.441
<i>What good is a flyby if we
can't get Watney off the surface?</i>

01:28:15.066 --> 01:28:17.903
Watney would intercept
using the MAV.

01:28:17.986 --> 01:28:19.070
Ow!

01:28:22.657 --> 01:28:24.618
And then we just head home.

01:28:29.080 --> 01:28:31.374
I've done the math.
It checks out.

01:28:33.168 --> 01:28:34.628
<i>Rich.
Yes, sir.</i>

01:28:34.711 --> 01:28:35.712
Get out.

01:28:35.837 --> 01:28:36.838
All right.

01:28:43.220 --> 01:28:44.971
- Is he right?
- Yeah.

01:28:45.305 --> 01:28:47.224
Bruce,
what do you think?

01:28:47.849 --> 01:28:49.351
<i>Well, if Vincent says so.</i>

01:28:50.769 --> 01:28:52.979
We need to use
the <i>Tat yang Shen?</i>

01:28:53.063 --> 01:28:54.064
Uh-huh.

01:28:54.314 --> 01:28:56.233
What am I missing?
Why is that important?

01:28:56.399 --> 01:28:57.692
Because we can only do one.

01:28:57.859 --> 01:29:00.028
Send Watney enough food
to last till <i>Ares 4...</i>

01:29:00.195 --> 01:29:02.113
or send <i>Hermes</i> back
to get him right now.

01:29:02.781 --> 01:29:03.990
Both plans require
the <i>Tat yang Shen,</i>

01:29:04.074 --> 01:29:05.867
so we have to choose.

01:29:06.284 --> 01:29:07.828
But what about
the <i>Hermes</i> crew?

01:29:07.994 --> 01:29:09.621
We'd be asking them to add

01:29:09.704 --> 01:29:12.791
533 extra days
to their mission.

01:29:13.083 --> 01:29:14.835
Yeah. They wouldn't hesitate,
not for a second.

01:29:15.919 --> 01:29:19.005
Can the <i>Hermes</i> function for 533
days beyond the scheduled mission end?

01:29:19.130 --> 01:29:20.131
It should.

01:29:20.298 --> 01:29:22.133
Built to last
the full <i>Ares</i> mission...

01:29:22.300 --> 01:29:24.386
so technically, it's only
halfway through its lifespan.

01:29:25.053 --> 01:29:26.221
But if something goes wrong...

01:29:26.388 --> 01:29:27.806
Then we lose the crew.

01:29:28.056 --> 01:29:29.724
<i>So what?
We either have a high chance</i>

01:29:29.808 --> 01:29:31.518
<i>of killing one person...</i>

01:29:31.810 --> 01:29:34.229
<i>or a low chance
of killing six people.</i>

01:29:34.396 --> 01:29:35.772
<i>How do we make
that decision?</i>

01:29:35.939 --> 01:29:37.732
We don't have to
make it, Bruce.

01:29:37.899 --> 01:29:38.900
He does.

01:29:39.234 --> 01:29:40.318
Yeah, well, bullshit.

01:29:40.819 --> 01:29:42.696
It should be
Commander Lewis' call.

01:29:42.863 --> 01:29:46.116
We still have a chance to bring
five astronauts home safe and sound.

01:29:46.199 --> 01:29:47.868
I'm not risking
their lives.

01:29:48.994 --> 01:29:50.662
Let them make
that decision.

01:29:51.371 --> 01:29:54.040
Mitch, we're going
with option one.

01:29:55.917 --> 01:29:57.961
You goddamn coward.

01:30:37.959 --> 01:30:40.712
So, he's been doing
EVAs throughout the day?

01:30:40.795 --> 01:30:43.632
But there's a pattern to
them. Here, watch this.

01:30:43.965 --> 01:30:46.801
So, he goes 300 meters
and then stops.

01:30:46.885 --> 01:30:48.553
Then he goes another
300 and stops.

01:30:50.096 --> 01:30:52.057
Nobody's giving him
any instructions?

01:30:52.140 --> 01:30:53.642
JPL didn't schedule
something?

01:30:53.725 --> 01:30:55.602
No. He's with the Rover.

01:30:57.520 --> 01:30:58.605
Okay, so we just got this.

01:31:00.273 --> 01:31:02.359
Chem analysis sample
batch 1A7C.

01:31:04.819 --> 01:31:07.447
Commander Lewis'
fuel compositing experiments.

01:31:10.742 --> 01:31:12.619
He's finishing the mission.

01:31:14.537 --> 01:31:15.664
Wow.

01:31:18.458 --> 01:31:22.045
We evac'd 18 sols
into a 31 -sol mission.

01:31:23.505 --> 01:31:24.756
Which means...

01:31:25.507 --> 01:31:29.761
we've got 13 sols' worth of
experiment and research left to do.

01:31:30.178 --> 01:31:32.013
So, Commander Lewis...

01:31:33.598 --> 01:31:35.350
your work is in good hands.

01:31:38.019 --> 01:31:39.145
Hey, Beck...

01:31:39.479 --> 01:31:42.774
I do not understand
chemolithotrophic detection.

01:31:42.857 --> 01:31:44.943
I mean, at all.

01:31:45.026 --> 01:31:48.989
But I'm working on it,
working my way through it.

01:31:49.531 --> 01:31:51.533
<i>Hey, Johanssen...</i>

01:31:53.535 --> 01:31:56.079
<i>I know you don't like it
when I touch the ChemCam.</i>

01:31:56.162 --> 01:31:57.330
<i>But...</i>

01:32:00.083 --> 01:32:02.460
<i>I'm touching the ChemCam.</i>

01:32:04.963 --> 01:32:06.089
<i>A lot.</i>

01:32:08.925 --> 01:32:10.677
Vogel, your core samples...

01:32:10.760 --> 01:32:12.762
in very good hands.

01:32:14.014 --> 01:32:15.432
Actually...

01:32:15.515 --> 01:32:18.059
I think I found
a new cataloguing system...

01:32:18.727 --> 01:32:19.978
<i>for the core samples.</i>

01:32:20.395 --> 01:32:24.524
<i>I've actually titled it</i>
"Das <i>Core Samples"</i>

01:32:24.607 --> 01:32:26.317
<i>out of respect
for the fatherland.</i>

01:32:28.611 --> 01:32:30.905
And finally, Martinez...

01:32:33.199 --> 01:32:35.869
<i>I'm still not sure
what it is that you do.</i>

01:32:37.579 --> 01:32:40.165
<i>Honest to God,
I really couldn't tell you.</i>

01:32:40.248 --> 01:32:42.083
<i>I don't know
why we brought you.</i>

01:32:42.542 --> 01:32:43.543
<i>That's it.</i>

01:32:44.127 --> 01:32:49.049
<i>Anyway, I'm trying to keep
everything documented, organized.</i>

01:32:49.716 --> 01:32:51.926
I know it's not
my strong suit...

01:32:52.469 --> 01:32:53.928
but I do want everything
to make sense.

01:32:54.012 --> 01:32:56.639
Just in case things
don't break my way.

01:32:58.183 --> 01:33:01.269
Actually, you could teach
all this in a class someday.

01:33:02.228 --> 01:33:05.940
<i>You know, the good stuff,
like how to make a bathtub...</i>

01:33:06.733 --> 01:33:09.486
<i>using NASA tubing
and an old RTG.</i>

01:33:09.569 --> 01:33:12.655
<i>How to cook a potato
about 6,000 different ways.</i>

01:33:13.490 --> 01:33:15.617
<i>The Mark Watney syllabus.</i>

01:33:30.632 --> 01:33:32.592
<i>Johanssen?
Yeah.</i>

01:33:33.301 --> 01:33:35.804
I know it's your private time.
Can I bother you for a second?

01:33:35.970 --> 01:33:36.971
<i>Yeah, go ahead.</i>

01:33:37.055 --> 01:33:38.056
<i>Where are you?</i>

01:33:38.139 --> 01:33:39.140
The gym.

01:34:00.966 --> 01:34:02.175
What's up?

01:34:02.342 --> 01:34:03.343
I just got an email
from my wife

01:34:03.468 --> 01:34:06.388
and the subject line
says, "Our children."

01:34:06.722 --> 01:34:08.724
My computer won't open
the attachment.

01:34:10.142 --> 01:34:11.351
Okay.

01:34:11.518 --> 01:34:12.644
Let's take a look.

01:34:16.565 --> 01:34:17.691
Uh...

01:34:19.151 --> 01:34:20.152
Let's see.

01:34:22.487 --> 01:34:24.489
This isn't a JPEG.

01:34:24.865 --> 01:34:27.325
It's a plain ASCll text file.

01:34:30.162 --> 01:34:31.413
I don't really know
what we're looking at.

01:34:33.165 --> 01:34:34.499
Does that make
any sense to you?

01:34:36.835 --> 01:34:39.004
"Rich Purnell Maneuver."

01:34:39.171 --> 01:34:42.007
It's a course maneuver
for the <i>Hermes.</i>

01:34:49.014 --> 01:34:50.223
And the mission concludes
with Earth intercept,

01:34:50.348 --> 01:34:51.349
211 days later.

01:34:52.726 --> 01:34:54.102
Would it work?

01:34:54.352 --> 01:34:56.354
Mmm-hmm. We ran
the numbers. They check out.

01:34:56.521 --> 01:34:57.939
It's a
brilliant course.

01:34:58.065 --> 01:35:00.901
So why all
the cloak and dagger?

01:35:01.068 --> 01:35:02.861
Because it goes directly
against NASA's decision.

01:35:03.028 --> 01:35:04.988
Yeah. If we do
the maneuver,

01:35:05.072 --> 01:35:07.199
they'd have to send
the supply ship or we die.

01:35:07.365 --> 01:35:10.577
We have the opportunity
to force their hand.

01:35:11.745 --> 01:35:14.081
So, are we gonna do it?

01:35:15.415 --> 01:35:17.375
If it was up to me,
we'd already be on our way.

01:35:17.542 --> 01:35:19.628
But it is, though,
isn't it? Up to you.

01:35:20.212 --> 01:35:21.296
Not this time.

01:35:21.463 --> 01:35:24.424
This is something NASA
expressly rejected.

01:35:25.258 --> 01:35:27.010
We're talking about
mutiny here,

01:35:27.094 --> 01:35:28.553
which is not a word
that I take lightly.

01:35:28.720 --> 01:35:31.264
So we do this together
or not at all.

01:35:31.431 --> 01:35:34.392
And before you answer,
consider the consequences.

01:35:34.559 --> 01:35:37.604
If we mess up the supply
rendezvous, we die.

01:35:37.771 --> 01:35:40.565
If we mess up the Earth
gravity assist, we die.

01:35:40.732 --> 01:35:43.777
If we do everything
perfectly...

01:35:43.944 --> 01:35:47.072
we add 533 days
to our mission.

01:35:47.239 --> 01:35:51.118
533 more days before
we see our families again.

01:35:51.284 --> 01:35:55.789
533 days of unplanned
space travel...

01:35:55.956 --> 01:35:57.499
where anything could go wrong.

01:35:57.666 --> 01:36:01.086
If it's mission
critical, we die.

01:36:01.920 --> 01:36:02.963
Sign me up.

01:36:03.130 --> 01:36:04.589
All right, cowboy,
slow down.

01:36:04.756 --> 01:36:06.424
You and I, we're military.

01:36:06.591 --> 01:36:08.260
Chances are, we go home,
they'll court martial us.

01:36:08.343 --> 01:36:09.344
Yeah, there's that.

01:36:09.427 --> 01:36:10.554
And for the rest
of you guys...

01:36:10.637 --> 01:36:13.557
I guarantee they will never
send you back up here again.

01:36:13.640 --> 01:36:18.103
Good. So, if we go
for it, how would it work?

01:36:18.270 --> 01:36:19.813
I plot the course
and execute it.

01:36:19.980 --> 01:36:21.273
Remote override.

01:36:21.439 --> 01:36:22.607
They could take
over the <i>Hermes</i>

01:36:22.691 --> 01:36:23.942
from Mission Control.

01:36:25.152 --> 01:36:27.112
Can you disable it?

01:36:27.279 --> 01:36:29.364
<i>Hermes</i> has four redundant
flight computers...

01:36:29.531 --> 01:36:31.825
each connected to three
redundant comm systems.

01:36:31.992 --> 01:36:33.243
We can't shut down
the comms

01:36:33.326 --> 01:36:34.828
because we'd lose
telemetry and guidance.

01:36:34.995 --> 01:36:36.538
And we can't shut down
the computers

01:36:36.621 --> 01:36:38.290
because we need
to run the ship.

01:36:38.456 --> 01:36:41.793
I'd have to disable remote
override on each system.

01:36:41.960 --> 01:36:43.879
It's part of the OS, I'd have
to jump over the code.

01:36:44.045 --> 01:36:46.673
Okay, but, like, in English,
what would that mean?

01:36:48.717 --> 01:36:49.968
I can do it.

01:36:50.969 --> 01:36:52.012
Great.

01:36:53.680 --> 01:36:55.182
Well, it has
to be unanimous.

01:36:55.974 --> 01:37:00.645
If we do this, it'll be
over 900 days of space.

01:37:02.981 --> 01:37:06.401
That's more than enough
space for one life...

01:37:07.235 --> 01:37:08.486
so, yes.

01:37:08.987 --> 01:37:10.363
I vote yes.

01:37:12.657 --> 01:37:14.201
Let's go get him.

01:37:17.078 --> 01:37:18.079
Johanssen?

01:37:18.747 --> 01:37:19.748
Yes.

01:37:20.165 --> 01:37:21.166
Yeah!

01:37:45.368 --> 01:37:47.620
But how are you
gonna do that?

01:37:48.621 --> 01:37:51.249
Got an unscheduled status
update from <i>Hermes.</i>

01:37:51.416 --> 01:37:52.417
Roger. Read it out.

01:37:52.584 --> 01:37:55.211
Message reads,
"Houston, please be advised.

01:37:55.378 --> 01:37:57.213
"Rich Purnell
is a steely-eyed missile man."

01:37:57.297 --> 01:37:58.298
What?

01:37:58.381 --> 01:37:59.799
Flight, Guidance.

01:37:59.966 --> 01:38:01.759
<i>Hermes</i> is off-course.

01:38:01.926 --> 01:38:03.219
CAPCOM, advise <i>Hermes</i>
they're drifting.

01:38:03.303 --> 01:38:04.888
Guidance, get
a correction ready.

01:38:05.054 --> 01:38:06.055
Negative, Flight,

01:38:06.973 --> 01:38:07.724
it's not drift.
They've adjusted course.

01:38:07.891 --> 01:38:09.059
What the hell?

01:38:09.225 --> 01:38:12.437
Telemetry, any chance this is
instrumentation failure?

01:38:12.604 --> 01:38:14.105
Negative, Flight.

01:38:14.272 --> 01:38:15.357
Guidance, work at
how long they can stay

01:38:15.440 --> 01:38:16.983
on this course
before it's irreversible.

01:38:17.150 --> 01:38:18.735
Working
on that now, Flight.

01:38:18.902 --> 01:38:20.278
Hey.
Who's Rich Purnell?

01:38:21.112 --> 01:38:22.113
I dunno.

01:38:22.781 --> 01:38:24.741
Will somebody find out
who the hell Rich Purnell is?

01:38:25.450 --> 01:38:27.410
Annie will go before
the media this morning...

01:38:27.577 --> 01:38:29.120
and inform them
of NASA's decision

01:38:29.245 --> 01:38:31.414
to reroute
the <i>Hermes</i> to Mars.

01:38:32.248 --> 01:38:33.917
Sounds like a smart move.

01:38:34.918 --> 01:38:37.003
Considering the circumstances.

01:38:37.754 --> 01:38:42.342
Whoever gave them the maneuver
only passed along information.

01:38:42.509 --> 01:38:44.302
Crew made the decision
on their own.

01:38:46.096 --> 01:38:47.931
You may have
killed them, Mitch.

01:38:50.100 --> 01:38:51.810
We're fighting the same war.

01:38:51.976 --> 01:38:53.395
Every time
something goes wrong,

01:38:53.478 --> 01:38:55.105
the world forgets why we fly.

01:38:55.271 --> 01:38:56.856
I'm trying
to keep us airborne.

01:38:57.023 --> 01:38:58.942
It's bigger than one person.

01:39:00.985 --> 01:39:02.153
No.

01:39:02.612 --> 01:39:03.613
It's not.

01:39:04.948 --> 01:39:08.493
When this is over,
I'll expect your resignation.

01:39:11.955 --> 01:39:13.123
I understand.

01:39:13.456 --> 01:39:15.792
Bring our astronauts home.

01:39:19.003 --> 01:39:21.714
Every <i>Ares</i> mission requires
three years of pre-supplies.

01:39:21.881 --> 01:39:24.467
So NASA decided a long time
ago it's a lot easier...

01:39:24.634 --> 01:39:25.844
to send some
of the stuff beforehand

01:39:25.969 --> 01:39:27.220
rather than bring it with us.

01:39:27.387 --> 01:39:31.057
So, as a result,
the MAV for <i>Ares 4...</i>

01:39:31.224 --> 01:39:32.308
is already there

01:39:32.392 --> 01:39:34.018
at the Schiaparelli Crater,
just waiting.

01:39:34.352 --> 01:39:38.189
So the plan is for me to use
that to go into orbit...

01:39:38.356 --> 01:39:41.317
just as the <i>Hermes</i>
is passing...

01:39:41.484 --> 01:39:44.654
and I guess they catch me?

01:39:45.697 --> 01:39:47.365
In space.

01:39:50.535 --> 01:39:52.829
So, I've got 200 sols
to figure out...

01:39:52.996 --> 01:39:55.665
how to take everything here
that's keeping me alive...

01:39:55.832 --> 01:39:57.500
the oxygen at or,
the water reclaimer,

01:39:57.584 --> 01:39:59.085
the atmospheric regulator...

01:39:59.669 --> 01:40:01.504
<i>bring that all with me.</i>

01:40:01.671 --> 01:40:04.090
<i>And luckily,
I have the greatest minds</i>

01:40:04.174 --> 01:40:05.341
<i>on Planet Earth...</i>

01:40:05.508 --> 01:40:08.553
<i>really, all of the brainpower
on the entire planet...</i>

01:40:08.720 --> 01:40:10.513
<i>helping me with this endeavor.</i>

01:40:10.680 --> 01:40:12.223
<i>And so far
they've come up with...</i>

01:40:12.390 --> 01:40:13.600
<i>"Hey, why don't you
drill holes</i>

01:40:13.683 --> 01:40:14.851
<i>"on the roof of your Rover...</i>

01:40:15.018 --> 01:40:16.769
<i>"and hit it as hard as you can
with a rock?"</i>

01:40:18.271 --> 01:40:20.231
We're gonna get there.

01:40:40.460 --> 01:40:42.337
<i>Didn't know
what time it was</i>

01:40:42.420 --> 01:40:45.632
<i>And the lights were low</i>

01:40:45.715 --> 01:40:48.927
<i>I leaned back on my radio</i>

01:40:51.054 --> 01:40:53.723
<i>Some cat was la yin' down
some rock 'n' roll</i>

01:40:53.806 --> 01:40:55.225
<i>Lotta soul, he said</i>

01:40:57.852 --> 01:41:02.190
<i>Then the loud sound
did seem to fade</i>

01:41:02.273 --> 01:41:06.486
<i>Came back like a slow voice
on a wave of phase</i>

01:41:06.569 --> 01:41:11.908
<i>That weren't no DJ
That was hazy cosmic jive</i>

01:41:15.620 --> 01:41:19.624
<i>There's a star man
waiting in the sky</i>

01:41:19.791 --> 01:41:22.085
<i>533 days longer?</i>

01:41:22.252 --> 01:41:24.087
<i>And you said yes to this?</i>

01:41:24.254 --> 01:41:26.130
He would have done the same
for me. You know that.

01:41:32.679 --> 01:41:34.305
Cheese. You do "cheese"?

01:41:34.430 --> 01:41:35.431
Cheese.

01:41:37.267 --> 01:41:38.268
He didn't do "cheese."

01:41:38.434 --> 01:41:39.644
Did he do "cheese"?

01:41:47.777 --> 01:41:48.945
<i>Hey, baby.</i>

01:41:49.070 --> 01:41:50.029
Hey.

01:41:50.113 --> 01:41:51.781
<i>I got something for you.</i>

01:41:51.948 --> 01:41:53.366
<i>Found it in the flea market.</i>

01:41:53.533 --> 01:41:54.951
<i>Original pressing.</i>

01:41:55.994 --> 01:41:56.995
No!

01:41:58.454 --> 01:41:59.539
<i>Not a scratch.</i>

01:41:59.706 --> 01:42:00.957
I love it.

01:42:01.124 --> 01:42:04.294
All due respect
to your CNSA protocol...

01:42:04.460 --> 01:42:06.796
but we haven't
done things that way...

01:42:06.963 --> 01:42:08.715
since <i>Apollo 9.</i>

01:42:10.383 --> 01:42:11.384
Did he get that?

01:42:16.973 --> 01:42:21.394
<i>Look out your window
I can see his light</i>

01:42:21.477 --> 01:42:24.355
<i>If we can sparkle
He may land tonight</i>

01:42:26.024 --> 01:42:27.817
Mmm-hmm.
Yeah. Mmm-hmm.

01:42:30.903 --> 01:42:31.904
Mmm-hmm.

01:42:33.990 --> 01:42:34.991
Oh!

01:42:35.074 --> 01:42:39.454
<i>There's a star man
waiting in the sky</i>

01:42:41.372 --> 01:42:42.624
<i>He'd like to
come and meet us</i>

01:42:42.707 --> 01:42:44.417
<i>But he thinks
he'd blow our minds</i>

01:42:44.667 --> 01:42:47.920
<i>There's a star man
waiting in the sky</i>

01:42:51.007 --> 01:42:52.008
<i>He's told us not to blow it</i>

01:42:52.091 --> 01:42:54.052
<i>'Cause he knows
it's all worthwhile</i>

01:42:54.177 --> 01:42:57.347
<i>He told me
Let the children lose it</i>

01:42:57.430 --> 01:43:01.184
<i>Let the children use it
Let all the children boogie</i>

