01:45:21.657 --> 01:45:24.327
<i>I've been thinking
about laws on Mars.</i>

01:45:24.994 --> 01:45:26.204
<i>There's an
international treaty</i>

01:45:26.329 --> 01:45:27.497
<i>saying no country
can lay claim...</i>

01:45:27.663 --> 01:45:29.040
<i>to anything that's
not on Earth.</i>

01:45:29.207 --> 01:45:30.291
<i>And by another treaty,</i>

01:45:30.374 --> 01:45:32.335
<i>if you're not in any
country's territory...</i>

01:45:32.502 --> 01:45:33.669
<i>maritime law applies.</i>

01:45:33.836 --> 01:45:36.714
<i>So Mars
is international waters.</i>

01:45:37.551 --> 01:45:40.887
<i>Now, NASA is an American
non-military organization.</i>

01:45:41.013 --> 01:45:42.014
<i>It owns the Hab.</i>

01:45:42.180 --> 01:45:44.891
<i>But the second I walk outside,
I'm in international waters.</i>

01:45:45.058 --> 01:45:46.393
<i>So here's the cool part.</i>

01:45:46.560 --> 01:45:48.061
<i>I'm about to leave for
the Schiaparelli Crater...</i>

01:45:48.228 --> 01:45:50.188
where I'm gonna commandeer
the <i>Ares 4</i> lander.

01:45:50.272 --> 01:45:53.275
Nobody explicitly gave me
permission to do this...

01:45:53.442 --> 01:45:55.569
<i>and they can't
until I'm on board the</i> Ares 4.

01:45:55.861 --> 01:45:58.196
<i>So that means I'm gonna be
taking a craft over...</i>

01:45:58.363 --> 01:46:00.574
<i>in international waters
without permission.</i>

01:46:00.741 --> 01:46:04.536
<i>Which, by definition,
makes me a pirate.</i>

01:46:05.579 --> 01:46:09.291
<i>Mark Watney,
Space Pirate.</i>

01:46:38.820 --> 01:46:40.489
A Space Pirate.

01:47:41.341 --> 01:47:45.053
<i>Everywhere I go,
I'm the first.</i>

01:47:45.720 --> 01:47:47.514
<i>It's a strange feeling.</i>

01:47:48.181 --> 01:47:49.724
<i>Step outside the Rover...</i>

01:47:49.891 --> 01:47:52.018
<i>first guy to be there.</i>

01:47:52.185 --> 01:47:55.355
<i>Climb that hill,
first guy to do that.</i>

01:47:56.356 --> 01:47:58.233
<i>Four and a half
billion years...</i>

01:47:58.400 --> 01:48:00.068
<i>nobody here.</i>

01:48:00.652 --> 01:48:03.363
<i>And now, me.</i>

01:48:04.698 --> 01:48:08.201
<i>I'm the first person to be
alone on an entire planet.</i>

01:48:23.383 --> 01:48:24.718
How's he doing?

01:48:26.386 --> 01:48:28.096
So far, so good.

01:48:28.263 --> 01:48:30.432
He's sticking to schedule.

01:48:30.599 --> 01:48:33.059
Drives for four hours
before noon...

01:48:33.727 --> 01:48:34.978
lays the solar panels,

01:48:35.061 --> 01:48:36.855
waits 13 hours for them
to recharge...

01:48:37.022 --> 01:48:41.359
and sleeps somewhere in there
and then starts again.

01:48:43.570 --> 01:48:44.571
How's he doing?

01:48:46.781 --> 01:48:49.618
He asked us to call him
Captain Blondebeard.

01:48:51.620 --> 01:48:53.455
Well, technically, Mars
would be under maritime...

01:48:53.622 --> 01:48:55.081
Yeah, I know.
He explained it to us.

01:48:57.167 --> 01:48:58.168
Where is he?

01:48:59.461 --> 01:49:00.754
There.
Okay.

01:49:01.046 --> 01:49:02.047
Here.

01:49:02.547 --> 01:49:03.548
Okay.

01:50:48.153 --> 01:50:50.322
<i>Okay, I'm gonna start
by stating for the record</i>

01:50:50.405 --> 01:50:51.781
<i>that you're not
gonna like this.</i>

01:50:52.115 --> 01:50:53.199
Oh, yeah?

01:50:53.950 --> 01:50:56.494
Yeah, the problem is
the intercept velocity.

01:50:56.661 --> 01:51:01.249
The <i>Hermes,</i> well...
It can't enter Mars' orbit.

01:51:01.416 --> 01:51:04.711
Otherwise, they'll never have
enough fuel to make it home.

01:51:04.878 --> 01:51:07.005
The MAV, that was
only designed

01:51:07.088 --> 01:51:09.382
to get to low Mars orbit.

01:51:09.549 --> 01:51:13.553
So in order for Mark to
escape Mars' gravity entirely...

01:51:13.720 --> 01:51:15.096
and to intercept
the <i>Hermes...</i>

01:51:15.263 --> 01:51:17.015
He has to be
going fast. Exactly.

01:51:17.182 --> 01:51:19.434
<i>Which means we need to make
the MAV lighter.</i>

01:51:19.601 --> 01:51:22.103
<i>A lot lighter.
5,000 kilograms lighter.</i>

01:51:22.395 --> 01:51:23.480
You can do that, right?

01:51:23.647 --> 01:51:25.440
<i>There's some gimmes
right off the bat.</i>

01:51:25.607 --> 01:51:28.526
<i>The design presumes 500
kilograms of Martian soil and samples.</i>

01:51:28.902 --> 01:51:30.320
Obviously we won't do that.

01:51:30.612 --> 01:51:34.240
And there's just one
passenger instead of six.

01:51:34.407 --> 01:51:37.077
With suits and gear,
that's another 500?

01:51:37.243 --> 01:51:39.120
Ditch the life support,
don't need it.

01:51:39.287 --> 01:51:41.081
And we'll get Mark to wear
his EVA suit the whole trip.

01:51:41.247 --> 01:51:42.916
Wait a second.
If he's in his EVA suit,

01:51:42.999 --> 01:51:44.376
how is he gonna
operate the controls?

01:51:44.459 --> 01:51:46.628
Well, he won't.

01:51:46.795 --> 01:51:50.423
Martinez will pilot the MAV
remotely from the <i>Hermes.</i>

01:51:50.590 --> 01:51:53.176
We've never had a manned ship
controlled remotely before.

01:51:54.719 --> 01:51:57.055
But I am excited about

01:51:57.138 --> 01:51:59.140
the opportunities
that affords.

01:51:59.307 --> 01:52:01.726
If we go remote, we can
lose the control panels,

01:52:01.810 --> 01:52:03.645
the secondary and tertiary
comm systems.

01:52:03.728 --> 01:52:04.771
<i>Wait a second.</i>

01:52:04.896 --> 01:52:08.817
<i>You want a remote-controlled
ascent with no backup comms?</i>

01:52:10.944 --> 01:52:13.571
He's not even got to
the bad stuff yet, Vincent.

01:52:13.738 --> 01:52:15.073
Let's skip to
the bad stuff!

01:52:15.448 --> 01:52:17.283
We need to remove
the nose airlock,

01:52:17.367 --> 01:52:20.161
the windows,
and Hull Panel 19.

01:52:21.788 --> 01:52:23.081
You want to take
the front of the ship off?

01:52:23.248 --> 01:52:24.374
<i>Sure.</i>

01:52:24.541 --> 01:52:26.918
<i>The nose airlock alone
is 400 kilograms.</i>

01:52:27.544 --> 01:52:29.879
You want to send a man into
space without the front of his ship?

01:52:30.046 --> 01:52:31.214
<i>Well, no.</i>

01:52:32.215 --> 01:52:35.385
<i>We're gonna have him cover it
with Hab canvas.</i>

01:52:36.219 --> 01:52:39.848
<i>Look, the hull's mostly there
to keep the air in.</i>

01:52:40.014 --> 01:52:41.516
Mars' atmosphere is so thin,

01:52:41.599 --> 01:52:43.059
you don't need
a lot of streamlining.

01:52:43.143 --> 01:52:44.769
By the time the ship's
going fast enough

01:52:44.853 --> 01:52:46.479
for air resistance
to matter...

01:52:46.646 --> 01:52:49.190
it'll be high enough that
there's practically no air.

01:52:49.274 --> 01:52:51.818
You wanna send him
into space under a tarp.

01:52:51.985 --> 01:52:53.319
<i>Yes.</i>

01:52:54.154 --> 01:52:55.864
<i>Can I go on?</i>

01:52:56.030 --> 01:52:57.115
No.

01:53:03.496 --> 01:53:05.915
You think
he means it like...

01:53:06.082 --> 01:53:07.709
"Are you kidding me?"

01:53:08.126 --> 01:53:09.169
You know?
Mmm-hmm.

01:53:09.502 --> 01:53:12.630
Or like,
"Are you kidding me?"

01:53:13.381 --> 01:53:15.133
I think it might be
the second one.

01:53:15.300 --> 01:53:16.634
Really?
Uh-huh.

01:53:16.926 --> 01:53:18.344
Could be the first way.

01:53:19.179 --> 01:53:20.221
"Are you kidding me?"

01:53:21.347 --> 01:53:22.974
Yeah, it could be
the first way.

01:55:20.633 --> 01:55:23.094
<i>I know
what they're doing.</i>

01:55:23.845 --> 01:55:25.930
I know exactly
what they're doing.

01:55:26.598 --> 01:55:28.308
They just keep repeating...

01:55:28.391 --> 01:55:31.311
"Go faster than any man in
the history of space travel."

01:55:31.769 --> 01:55:32.812
<i>Like that's a good thing.</i>

01:55:32.979 --> 01:55:35.273
<i>Like it'll distract me from
how insane their plan is.</i>

01:55:36.691 --> 01:55:38.151
Yeah, I get to go
faster than any man

01:55:38.276 --> 01:55:40.278
in the history
of space travel...

01:55:40.445 --> 01:55:43.323
because you are launching me
in a convertible.

01:55:43.489 --> 01:55:45.074
Actually it's worse than that

01:55:45.158 --> 01:55:46.993
because I won't even be able
to control the thing.

01:55:47.201 --> 01:55:49.954
<i>And by the way, physicists,</i>

01:55:50.038 --> 01:55:52.790
<i>when describing things
like acceleration...</i>

01:55:52.957 --> 01:55:55.001
<i>do not use the word "fast."</i>

01:55:55.835 --> 01:55:57.921
So they're only doing that
in the hopes

01:55:58.004 --> 01:56:00.214
that I won't raise
any objections...

01:56:01.215 --> 01:56:03.343
to this lunacy.

01:56:04.177 --> 01:56:05.637
<i>Because I like the way</i>

01:56:05.720 --> 01:56:08.681
<i>"fastest man in the history
of space travel" sounds.</i>

01:56:10.516 --> 01:56:12.518
<i>I do like the way it sounds.</i>

01:56:13.686 --> 01:56:15.521
<i>I mean, I like it a lot.</i>

01:56:18.024 --> 01:56:20.151
<i>I'm not gonna tell them that.</i>

01:56:26.199 --> 01:56:27.241
<i>Okay.</i>

01:56:28.868 --> 01:56:30.662
<i>Let's do this.</i>

01:56:36.417 --> 01:56:37.877
<i>My, my</i>

01:56:38.086 --> 01:56:42.048
<i>At Waterloo
Napoleon did surrender</i>

01:56:43.049 --> 01:56:44.050
<i>Oh, yeah</i>

01:56:44.717 --> 01:56:50.223
<i>And I have met my destiny
in quite a similar way</i>

01:56:51.265 --> 01:56:57.063
<i>The history book on the
shelf is always repeating itself</i>

01:56:59.899 --> 01:57:01.234
<i>Waterloo</i>

01:57:01.359 --> 01:57:04.237
<i>I was defeated
You won the war</i>

01:57:05.571 --> 01:57:06.906
Come on.

01:57:07.240 --> 01:57:10.702
<i>Waterloo, promise to
love you for evermore</i>

01:57:13.746 --> 01:57:17.125
<i>Waterloo
Couldn't escape if I wanted to</i>

01:57:20.378 --> 01:57:21.462
<i>Waterloo</i>

01:57:21.587 --> 01:57:23.256
<i>Knowing my fate
is to be with you</i>

01:57:23.923 --> 01:57:26.384
<i>Oh, oh, oh, oh, Waterloo</i>

01:57:27.260 --> 01:57:29.929
<i>Finally facing my Waterloo</i>

01:57:34.142 --> 01:57:35.226
<i>My, my</i>

01:57:36.436 --> 01:57:39.063
<i>I tried to hold you back</i>

01:57:39.147 --> 01:57:40.940
<i>But you were stronger</i>

01:57:41.566 --> 01:57:42.692
<i>Oh, yeah</i>

01:57:43.401 --> 01:57:48.031
<i>And now it seems my only
chance is giving up the fight</i>

01:57:49.949 --> 01:57:52.410
<i>And how could I ever refuse?</i>

01:57:53.119 --> 01:57:55.955
<i>I feel like I
win when I lose</i>

01:57:58.166 --> 01:57:59.459
<i>Waterloo</i>

01:58:00.001 --> 01:58:02.628
<i>I was defeated
You won the war</i>

01:58:04.797 --> 01:58:06.299
<i>Waterloo</i>

01:58:06.924 --> 01:58:09.343
<i>Promise to love you
for evermore</i>

01:58:11.429 --> 01:58:12.430
<i>Waterloo</i>

01:58:12.930 --> 01:58:15.308
<i>Couldn't escape
if I wanted to</i>

01:58:18.436 --> 01:58:19.520
<i>Waterloo</i>

01:58:19.645 --> 01:58:22.356
<i>Knowing my fate
is to be with you</i>

01:58:23.024 --> 01:58:24.984
<i>Oh, oh, oh, oh, Waterloo</i>

01:58:26.110 --> 01:58:29.322
<i>Finally facing my Waterloo</i>

01:58:30.573 --> 01:58:33.659
<i>So how could I ever refuse?</i>

01:58:33.826 --> 01:58:37.497
<i>I feel like I
win when I lose</i>

01:58:37.580 --> 01:58:39.207
<i>Waterloo</i>

01:58:39.290 --> 01:58:42.085
<i>Couldn't escape
if I wanted to</i>

01:58:43.086 --> 01:58:44.754
And you killed him.

01:58:44.837 --> 01:58:46.172
Try it again.

01:58:49.050 --> 01:58:50.968
<i>Ooh, Waterloo</i>

01:58:52.095 --> 01:58:54.430
<i>Finally facing my Waterloo</i>

01:58:54.931 --> 01:58:58.101
<i>Oh, oh, oh, oh, Waterloo</i>

01:59:39.517 --> 01:59:41.519
How many times
you kill Watney?

01:59:42.311 --> 01:59:45.481
The important thing is that
I got all scenarios into orbit.

01:59:45.565 --> 01:59:46.566
That's the important thing.

01:59:48.901 --> 01:59:50.611
- He's ready.
- Okay.

01:59:52.530 --> 01:59:53.823
Here's the plan.

01:59:53.906 --> 01:59:55.575
Martinez flies the MAV.

01:59:55.950 --> 01:59:57.869
Johanssen sysops
the ascent.

01:59:57.952 --> 02:00:01.289
Beck, Vogel, I want you guys in
Airlock 2 with the outer door open...

02:00:01.414 --> 02:00:02.999
before the MAV
even launches. Okay.

02:00:03.082 --> 02:00:06.878
Once we hit intercept, it's
Beck's job to go get Watney.

02:00:07.753 --> 02:00:09.797
He may be in bad shape
when we get him.

02:00:09.881 --> 02:00:12.800
The stripped-down MAV will get
him to 12 G's during the launch.

02:00:12.884 --> 02:00:15.094
He could be knocked unconscious,
may even have internal bleeding.

02:00:15.303 --> 02:00:17.680
Well, it's a good thing
you're our doctor.

02:00:19.473 --> 02:00:20.641
What's the intercept plan?

02:00:21.601 --> 02:00:23.895
We finished attaching the
tethers into one long line.

02:00:24.061 --> 02:00:26.063
It's 214 meters long.

02:00:26.189 --> 02:00:28.566
And I'll have the MMU, so
moving around should be easy.

02:00:28.941 --> 02:00:30.860
How fast a relative velocity
can you handle?

02:00:31.944 --> 02:00:34.447
I can grab the MAV
at 5 meters per second.

02:00:34.614 --> 02:00:36.699
10 is like jumping
onto a moving train.

02:00:36.866 --> 02:00:38.743
So any more than that
and I might miss.

02:00:38.993 --> 02:00:40.828
Well, we may
have some leeway.

02:00:42.455 --> 02:00:44.123
The launch takes 12 minutes.

02:00:44.415 --> 02:00:47.543
And it'll be 52 minutes
before intercept.

02:00:47.835 --> 02:00:50.129
Once Mark's
engine shuts off...

02:00:50.296 --> 02:00:52.715
we'll know our intercept point
and velocity.

02:00:52.882 --> 02:00:54.383
Vogel, you're Beck's backup.

02:00:54.550 --> 02:00:57.220
Everything goes well, you pull
them on board with the tether.

02:00:57.386 --> 02:01:00.139
If anything goes wrong,
you go out after him.

02:01:00.264 --> 02:01:01.307
Yep.

02:01:01.390 --> 02:01:02.391
Okay.

02:01:04.810 --> 02:01:06.979
Let's go get our boy.

02:02:08.148 --> 02:02:10.275
There's a mood
of tension and anxiety here,

02:02:10.358 --> 02:02:11.693
outside Johnson Space Center.

02:02:11.860 --> 02:02:12.861
As you can see,

02:02:12.944 --> 02:02:15.238
many people
have gathered here...

02:02:15.405 --> 02:02:16.740
to see whether or not

02:02:16.823 --> 02:02:19.493
the mission to retrieve Mark
Watney will be a success.

02:02:19.826 --> 02:02:21.620
<i>They've had
some contact with him,</i>

02:02:21.703 --> 02:02:23.663
<i>but it hasn't been very much.</i>

02:02:23.747 --> 02:02:25.040
<i>We have to remind our viewers</i>

02:02:25.123 --> 02:02:26.833
<i>that we're watching this
as it unfolds.</i>

02:02:27.000 --> 02:02:28.794
<i>So we'll try
and keep you up-to-date</i>

02:02:28.877 --> 02:02:30.754
<i>as to what exactly
is going on.</i>

02:02:30.921 --> 02:02:34.591
<i>Let's listen in to NASA making
contact with Mark Watney.</i>

02:02:34.758 --> 02:02:35.967
<i>Let's listen in.</i>

02:02:42.641 --> 02:02:44.935
If something goes wrong,
what can Mission Control do?

02:02:45.393 --> 02:02:47.479
Not a damn thing.

02:02:48.021 --> 02:02:49.815
It's all happening
12 light-minutes away...

02:02:49.981 --> 02:02:51.650
which means it takes
24 minutes for them

02:02:51.733 --> 02:02:53.693
to get the answer to any
question they ask.

02:02:54.820 --> 02:02:56.863
The whole launch
is 12 minutes...

02:02:58.573 --> 02:03:00.200
so they're on their own.

02:04:05.432 --> 02:04:06.433
<i>...just how long
Mark has been</i>

02:04:06.516 --> 02:04:07.601
<i>completely alone on Mars.</i>

02:04:07.684 --> 02:04:10.645
<i>We're talking to a psychology
expert later to discuss...</i>

02:04:33.418 --> 02:04:35.003
<i>Fuel pressure,
green.</i>

02:04:35.378 --> 02:04:37.547
Engine alignment, perfect.

02:04:37.714 --> 02:04:38.965
<i>Communications, five by five.</i>

02:04:40.717 --> 02:04:43.511
<i>We are ready for pre-flight
checklist, Commander.</i>

02:04:43.678 --> 02:04:46.264
<i>Mission Control,
this is</i> Hermes <i>actual.</i>

02:04:46.348 --> 02:04:48.350
<i>We will proceed on schedule.</i>

02:04:48.516 --> 02:04:50.518
<i>We are T-minus 2 minutes,
10 seconds</i>

02:04:50.602 --> 02:04:52.020
<i>to launch, by the mark.</i>

02:04:53.271 --> 02:04:54.272
About two minutes, Watney.

02:04:54.439 --> 02:04:55.523
<i>How you doing down there?</i>

02:04:55.690 --> 02:04:56.900
I'm good.

02:04:57.734 --> 02:04:59.527
I'm anxious to get up to you.

02:05:00.070 --> 02:05:01.404
Thanks for coming back for me.

02:05:01.488 --> 02:05:02.489
<i>Well, we're on it.</i>

02:05:02.656 --> 02:05:04.366
<i>Remember, you'll be pulling
some serious G's,</i>

02:05:04.449 --> 02:05:06.117
<i>so it's okay to pass out.</i>

02:05:06.284 --> 02:05:08.245
You're in
Martinez's hands now.

02:05:08.954 --> 02:05:10.997
Well, tell that asshole
no barrel rolls.

02:05:12.457 --> 02:05:13.750
Copy that, MAV.

02:05:14.209 --> 02:05:15.210
<i>CAPCOM.</i>

02:05:15.335 --> 02:05:16.294
<i>Go.</i>

02:05:16.378 --> 02:05:17.420
<i>Remote command.</i>

02:05:17.504 --> 02:05:18.463
<i>Go.</i>

02:05:18.546 --> 02:05:19.547
<i>Recovery.</i>

02:05:19.631 --> 02:05:20.632
<i>Go.</i>

02:05:20.715 --> 02:05:22.133
<i>Secondary recovery.</i>

02:05:22.217 --> 02:05:23.218
<i>Go.</i>

02:05:23.343 --> 02:05:24.344
Pilot.

02:05:25.679 --> 02:05:26.680
Go.

02:05:26.805 --> 02:05:28.014
<i>Pilot.</i>

02:05:28.181 --> 02:05:29.307
<i>Go.</i>

02:05:29.474 --> 02:05:30.475
<i>Copy. We're go.</i>

02:05:31.643 --> 02:05:34.020
<i>Mission Control,
we are go for launch.</i>

02:05:34.854 --> 02:05:36.064
T-minus ten...

02:05:36.189 --> 02:05:37.148
nine...

02:05:37.232 --> 02:05:38.233
Main engine start.

02:05:38.400 --> 02:05:39.526
<i>eight...</i>

02:05:39.901 --> 02:05:40.902
<i>seven...</i>

02:05:41.069 --> 02:05:42.696
<i>Mooring clamps released.</i>

02:05:42.862 --> 02:05:44.447
About five seconds, Watney.
Hang on.

02:05:44.614 --> 02:05:46.283
I'll see you in a few,
Commander.

02:05:46.574 --> 02:05:47.909
<i>four...</i>

02:05:48.285 --> 02:05:49.577
<i>three...</i>

02:05:49.869 --> 02:05:51.037
<i>two...</i>

02:05:51.454 --> 02:05:52.497
<i>one.</i>

02:06:28.783 --> 02:06:31.161
Velocity,
741 meters per second.

02:06:31.328 --> 02:06:33.288
Altitude, 1350 meters.

02:06:33.455 --> 02:06:34.622
That's too low.

02:06:34.789 --> 02:06:35.915
It's fighting me.

02:06:39.794 --> 02:06:43.006
<i>Watney, do you read?</i>

02:06:49.012 --> 02:06:50.305
<i>Booster
separation complete.</i>

02:06:50.472 --> 02:06:53.475
Velocity, 850.
Altitude, 1843.

02:06:53.683 --> 02:06:54.893
He's well below
target altitude.

02:06:55.060 --> 02:06:56.311
How far below?

02:06:56.811 --> 02:06:57.896
Checking.

02:06:58.480 --> 02:06:59.689
Watney.

02:07:00.315 --> 02:07:01.316
Do you read?

02:07:01.608 --> 02:07:02.817
<i>He's probably
passed out.</i>

02:07:02.984 --> 02:07:04.152
<i>He pulled 12 g's
on the ascent.</i>

02:07:04.235 --> 02:07:05.487
<i>Give him a few minutes.</i>

02:07:05.653 --> 02:07:06.821
<i>Copy that.</i>

02:07:09.157 --> 02:07:10.492
<i>Main shutdown
in three...</i>

02:07:11.159 --> 02:07:12.744
<i>two, one...</i>

02:07:13.453 --> 02:07:14.537
<i>shutdown.</i>

02:07:16.081 --> 02:07:17.582
<i>Back to
automatic guidance.</i>

02:07:18.541 --> 02:07:19.876
<i>Shutdown confirmed.</i>

02:07:22.379 --> 02:07:24.130
<i>Fuel reserves depleted.</i>

02:07:26.049 --> 02:07:27.217
<i>Watney?</i>

02:07:27.801 --> 02:07:29.010
<i>Do you read?</i>

02:07:30.011 --> 02:07:31.221
<i>It seems there's some
sort of problem</i>

02:07:31.304 --> 02:07:32.680
<i>with the transmission.</i>

02:07:39.854 --> 02:07:41.398
<i>I have interval pings.</i>

02:07:41.564 --> 02:07:43.608
Intercept velocity will be
11 meters per second.

02:07:43.775 --> 02:07:45.527
I can make that work.

02:07:46.152 --> 02:07:48.488
Distance
at intercept will be...

02:07:48.947 --> 02:07:50.615
We'll be 68 kilometers apart.

02:07:51.074 --> 02:07:52.867
<i>68 kilometers?</i>

02:07:53.076 --> 02:07:55.537
<i>Did she just say
68 kilometers?</i>

02:07:56.287 --> 02:07:57.288
Come on, guys,
keep it together.

02:07:57.372 --> 02:07:58.415
Work the problem.

02:07:59.082 --> 02:08:00.708
Johanssen, time to intercept?

02:08:01.376 --> 02:08:03.378
<i>39 minutes,
12 seconds.</i>

02:08:04.045 --> 02:08:05.547
Martinez, what if we
point our thrusters

02:08:05.630 --> 02:08:06.756
in the same direction?

02:08:07.215 --> 02:08:10.176
Well, it depends how much fuel
you wanna save...

02:08:10.343 --> 02:08:11.678
for the altitude adjustments
for the trip home.

02:08:11.845 --> 02:08:12.929
How much do you need?

02:08:13.096 --> 02:08:17.392
I can get by with about 20%
of what we have left.

02:08:18.435 --> 02:08:21.688
If we use 75.5% of remaining
altitude-adjust fuel...

02:08:21.855 --> 02:08:23.731
that will bring the intercept
range to zero.

02:08:23.857 --> 02:08:24.858
Do it.

02:08:25.775 --> 02:08:28.903
Hang on. It brings
the range to zero...

02:08:29.070 --> 02:08:31.781
but the intercept velocity
will be 42 meters per second.

02:08:31.948 --> 02:08:33.575
<i>And that's way too fast.</i>

02:08:33.992 --> 02:08:35.994
Then we'll have 39 minutes
to figure out

02:08:36.077 --> 02:08:37.620
how to slow down.

02:08:40.707 --> 02:08:42.459
Martinez, burn the jets.

02:08:42.625 --> 02:08:44.002
Copy that.

02:09:06.441 --> 02:09:08.485
MAV to <i>Hermes.</i>

02:09:08.568 --> 02:09:09.569
<i>Watney?</i>

02:09:09.694 --> 02:09:10.695
<i>Affirmative.</i>

02:09:14.741 --> 02:09:16.159
<i>What's your status?</i>

02:09:16.534 --> 02:09:18.036
<i>My chest hurts.</i>

02:09:18.578 --> 02:09:19.954
<i>I broke my ribs.</i>

02:09:20.788 --> 02:09:21.873
<i>How are you?</i>

02:09:22.040 --> 02:09:23.124
<i>We're working on
getting to you.</i>

02:09:23.208 --> 02:09:24.709
<i>There was a complication
during launch.</i>

02:09:24.876 --> 02:09:26.252
Copy that.

02:09:26.794 --> 02:09:28.254
How bad is it?

02:09:28.421 --> 02:09:29.964
<i>We've corrected
the intercept range,</i>

02:09:30.048 --> 02:09:32.008
<i>but we've got a problem
with intercept velocity.</i>

02:09:32.175 --> 02:09:33.468
<i>How big a problem?</i>

02:09:33.635 --> 02:09:35.136
42 meters a second.

02:09:35.512 --> 02:09:36.513
Well...

02:09:37.805 --> 02:09:39.265
shit.

02:09:44.187 --> 02:09:45.980
<i>Commander, I have an idea.</i>

02:09:46.147 --> 02:09:47.565
<i>Go ahead, Mark.</i>

02:09:47.732 --> 02:09:50.902
Well, if I can find
something sharp in here...

02:09:51.069 --> 02:09:53.905
and poke a hole in the glove
of my EVA suit...

02:09:54.072 --> 02:09:56.533
I could use the escaping air
as a thruster...

02:09:56.699 --> 02:09:58.076
<i>and fly towards you.</i>

02:09:58.243 --> 02:10:01.287
<i>It would be easy to control
because it would be on my arm.</i>

02:10:01.579 --> 02:10:03.414
I can't see you having
any control if you did that.

02:10:03.581 --> 02:10:05.375
You'd be eyeballing
the intercept

02:10:05.458 --> 02:10:07.752
using a thrust vector
you can barely control.

02:10:07.961 --> 02:10:11.130
Yes, those are all
very good points.

02:10:11.923 --> 02:10:14.676
But consider this.

02:10:16.719 --> 02:10:19.389
I'd get to fly around
like Iron Man.

02:10:20.682 --> 02:10:22.267
<i>Commander...</i>

02:10:22.433 --> 02:10:24.102
<i>let's go Iron Man.</i>

02:10:27.188 --> 02:10:28.439
<i>This is unexpected IOS.</i>

02:10:28.690 --> 02:10:30.567
<i>Communication lost. Stand by.</i>

02:10:31.609 --> 02:10:34.237
Maybe it's not the worst idea.

02:10:34.404 --> 02:10:36.239
No, it is the worst idea.
It's the worst idea ever.

02:10:36.406 --> 02:10:37.532
Not what he said.

02:10:37.699 --> 02:10:39.826
Using atmosphere as thrust.

02:10:42.036 --> 02:10:43.955
What happens
if we blow the VAL?

02:10:44.247 --> 02:10:46.040
Wait, you want to open
the vehicular airlock?

02:10:46.291 --> 02:10:47.375
It'll give us a good kick.

02:10:47.542 --> 02:10:49.294
But also blow
the nose off the ship.

02:10:49.460 --> 02:10:52.255
And all the air would leave
and we need air to not die.

02:10:52.422 --> 02:10:54.674
We would seal the bridge
and the reactor room.

02:10:54.841 --> 02:10:56.092
Everywhere else
would go vacuo.

02:10:56.718 --> 02:10:57.719
Vogel.

02:10:57.802 --> 02:10:59.095
<i>Go ahead, Commander.</i>

02:10:59.512 --> 02:11:02.140
I need you to come inside
and make a... bomb.

02:11:02.890 --> 02:11:03.850
Again, Commander?

02:11:03.933 --> 02:11:04.934
<i>You're a chemist.</i>

02:11:05.018 --> 02:11:06.853
<i>Can you make a bomb with
what you have on board?</i>

02:11:08.521 --> 02:11:09.814
Probably.

02:11:10.898 --> 02:11:12.191
But I feel obliged to mention

02:11:12.275 --> 02:11:14.068
that setting off
an explosive device...

02:11:14.235 --> 02:11:16.988
<i>in a spacecraft
is a terrible, terrible idea.</i>

02:11:17.322 --> 02:11:20.199
Hang on. You guys making
a bomb without me?

02:11:20.366 --> 02:11:22.368
<i>Copy that.
Can you do it?</i>

02:11:24.787 --> 02:11:25.788
<i>Ja.</i>

02:11:28.708 --> 02:11:30.293
<i>Houston, be advised.</i>

02:11:30.460 --> 02:11:31.919
<i>We are going to deliberately
breach the VAL</i>

02:11:32.003 --> 02:11:33.546
<i>to produce a reverse thrust.</i>

02:11:34.756 --> 02:11:37.175
Beck, leave your suit on.
Meet Johanssen in Airlock 1.

02:11:37.342 --> 02:11:38.384
We'll open the outer door.

02:11:38.551 --> 02:11:40.219
<i>I need you to place the charge
on the inner door.</i>

02:11:40.678 --> 02:11:42.805
<i>Climb back to Airlock 2
along the hull.</i>

02:11:42.972 --> 02:11:44.807
Copy. I'm on my way.

02:11:52.065 --> 02:11:53.524
I'm in, Commander.

02:11:53.691 --> 02:11:54.734
<i>Copy that.</i>

02:12:03.910 --> 02:12:05.495
Vogel, where are you?

02:12:05.662 --> 02:12:06.704
<i>I'm in the kitchen.</i>

02:12:06.871 --> 02:12:07.872
Okay.

02:12:11.876 --> 02:12:12.877
<i>Commander...</i>

02:12:13.044 --> 02:12:14.921
I can't let you
go through with this.

02:12:15.088 --> 02:12:16.673
I am prepared to cut the suit.

02:12:16.964 --> 02:12:18.007
Absolutely not.

02:12:18.174 --> 02:12:20.593
<i>See, the thing is,
I'm selfish.</i>

02:12:20.802 --> 02:12:23.680
I want all the memorials back
home to be about me.

02:12:23.763 --> 02:12:24.764
<i>Just me.</i>

02:12:24.889 --> 02:12:27.183
I should have left
this guy on Mars.

02:12:27.475 --> 02:12:28.518
Hey.

02:12:31.396 --> 02:12:32.397
Sugar?

02:12:32.480 --> 02:12:33.481
Yeah.

02:12:37.610 --> 02:12:39.028
Can you hold this?

02:12:39.612 --> 02:12:41.322
Liquid oxygen...

02:12:41.489 --> 02:12:44.992
and some stain remover
that contains ammonia.

02:12:48.496 --> 02:12:51.791
This thing here
is five times stronger

02:12:51.874 --> 02:12:53.292
than a stick of dynamite.

02:12:53.376 --> 02:12:54.627
How do we activate it?

02:12:54.794 --> 02:12:57.547
Can you connect this to
one of our lighting panels?

02:13:01.092 --> 02:13:02.385
Careful.

02:13:08.933 --> 02:13:10.435
Open Airlock 1.

02:13:16.274 --> 02:13:17.483
I'm on
the way to Beck.

02:13:21.738 --> 02:13:23.614
Let's just hope
this is a good idea, guys.

02:13:23.698 --> 02:13:24.741
It is.

02:13:24.824 --> 02:13:26.033
<i>Open A 1.</i>

02:13:31.372 --> 02:13:32.373
Hi.
Hey.

02:13:36.043 --> 02:13:37.587
You got it?
Mmm.

02:13:43.217 --> 02:13:45.845
Make sure you're not in here
when this thing goes off.

02:13:47.638 --> 02:13:48.848
Hey.

02:13:49.015 --> 02:13:50.850
Be careful out there.

02:13:51.017 --> 02:13:52.185
In space.

02:13:56.272 --> 02:13:58.649
Don't tell anyone
I did that.

02:14:08.534 --> 02:14:09.577
Bomb set.

02:14:18.336 --> 02:14:20.713
Leaving Airlock 1.

02:14:25.635 --> 02:14:26.761
Guys,
I'm running the numbers,

02:14:26.844 --> 02:14:28.012
and even with
optimal VAL blow...

02:14:28.304 --> 02:14:29.847
we're gonna be off
on our angle.

02:14:30.181 --> 02:14:32.850
What's the intercept
distance? Johanssen?

02:14:33.017 --> 02:14:35.353
260 meters, approximate.

02:14:35.520 --> 02:14:36.813
That's too far.

02:14:45.863 --> 02:14:47.114
Commander?

02:14:47.281 --> 02:14:49.158
Martinez, close the door.

02:14:52.662 --> 02:14:53.955
Open D3.

02:14:54.497 --> 02:14:56.332
And leave it open.

02:15:25.027 --> 02:15:26.696
<i>Open B2.</i>

02:15:30.908 --> 02:15:32.118
Johanssen...

02:15:32.285 --> 02:15:34.620
<i>time to VAL blow
after initiate?</i>

02:15:34.787 --> 02:15:35.955
15 seconds.

02:15:36.205 --> 02:15:38.249
We sure know
how to cut it close.

02:15:39.166 --> 02:15:40.209
Commander?

02:15:40.293 --> 02:15:42.253
Distance is too far.
I'm going out.

02:15:42.837 --> 02:15:43.838
I can do this.

02:15:43.963 --> 02:15:44.964
It's not a debate.

02:15:45.089 --> 02:15:46.632
I'm not risking
another crew member.

02:15:47.049 --> 02:15:48.551
Beck's returned.

02:15:48.718 --> 02:15:50.511
Johanssen, initiate the bomb.

02:15:50.845 --> 02:15:51.971
Ten seconds.

02:15:52.179 --> 02:15:53.180
Strap in.

02:15:53.848 --> 02:15:54.849
<i>Five, four...</i>

02:15:56.684 --> 02:15:57.685
Three...

02:15:57.768 --> 02:15:59.478
Brace for deceleration.
<i>two...</i>

02:16:00.021 --> 02:16:01.022
one.

02:16:01.147 --> 02:16:02.481
Activating Panel 41.

02:16:24.503 --> 02:16:25.713
Bridge seal holding.

02:16:25.880 --> 02:16:26.923
What's the damage?

02:16:27.089 --> 02:16:28.257
<i>Worry about that later.</i>

02:16:28.424 --> 02:16:29.967
<i>What's the relative velocity?</i>

02:16:30.134 --> 02:16:31.218
12 meters per second.

02:16:31.636 --> 02:16:32.637
<i>Copy.</i>

02:16:35.222 --> 02:16:36.223
Hook me up.

02:16:37.975 --> 02:16:39.185
Done.

02:16:49.111 --> 02:16:50.863
I have visual on the MAV.

02:16:52.239 --> 02:16:53.866
<i>What's the intercept range?</i>

02:16:54.158 --> 02:16:55.409
I'm checking.

02:16:56.369 --> 02:16:57.828
312 meters.

02:16:57.995 --> 02:17:00.331
Did you say 312?

02:17:00.498 --> 02:17:03.000
Great. I'll wave at you guys
as I go by.

02:17:06.462 --> 02:17:07.463
Oh!

02:17:12.134 --> 02:17:14.220
I can't get to you, Mark.
You're too far.

02:17:14.345 --> 02:17:15.346
<i>I'm not gonna make it.</i>

02:17:15.429 --> 02:17:16.430
I know.

02:17:16.514 --> 02:17:17.974
<i>Beck, unhook me.
I'm going after him.</i>

02:17:18.516 --> 02:17:21.018
Commander, I got this.

02:17:34.949 --> 02:17:36.450
Mark, report.

02:17:36.701 --> 02:17:38.285
On my way, Commander.

02:17:38.703 --> 02:17:39.787
Damn it.

02:17:49.630 --> 02:17:51.882
Johanssen, what's my relative
velocity to Mark?

02:17:52.133 --> 02:17:53.926
5.2 meters per second.

02:17:54.176 --> 02:17:55.720
Copy. Adjusting course.

02:18:01.475 --> 02:18:03.227
<i>3. 1 meters
per second.</i>

02:18:05.896 --> 02:18:07.982
<i>Distance to target, 24 meters.</i>

02:18:09.859 --> 02:18:11.694
11 meters to target.

02:18:14.572 --> 02:18:15.573
<i>Six meters.</i>

02:18:25.750 --> 02:18:28.127
Hold on, Mark.

02:18:56.113 --> 02:18:57.114
I got him.

02:19:00.034 --> 02:19:01.285
<i>I got him.</i>

02:19:02.203 --> 02:19:03.245
<i>I got him.</i>

02:19:03.621 --> 02:19:05.206
Way to go, Iron Man.

02:19:08.375 --> 02:19:09.585
Beck, pull us in.

02:19:12.755 --> 02:19:14.298
It's good
to see you.

02:19:16.175 --> 02:19:17.218
You...

02:19:19.011 --> 02:19:22.306
have terrible taste in music.

02:19:32.066 --> 02:19:34.068
<i>Houston,
this is</i> Hermes <i>actual.</i>

02:19:34.193 --> 02:19:35.194
<i>We got him.</i>

02:19:37.238 --> 02:19:38.614
<i>Watney is secure.</i>

02:19:43.911 --> 02:19:45.996
Contact in outer
space with Mark Watney.

02:19:46.163 --> 02:19:48.165
<i>After a very long time,
they have done</i>

02:19:48.249 --> 02:19:50.334
<i>what many people thought
was impossible.</i>

02:19:50.501 --> 02:19:51.710
<i>Mission confirmation:</i>

02:19:51.794 --> 02:19:54.171
<i>Mark Watney has been
successfully rescued.</i>

02:19:58.092 --> 02:19:59.760
Grab a hold of him.

02:20:01.762 --> 02:20:02.972
Hey, handsome!

02:20:03.139 --> 02:20:04.765
Beck, close the hatch.

02:20:07.977 --> 02:20:09.311
Hey, guys!

02:20:14.024 --> 02:20:16.152
<i>Houston,
six crew safely aboard.</i>

02:20:20.573 --> 02:20:23.659
<i>This is huge moment for
this nation, for the world...</i>

02:20:23.826 --> 02:20:26.954
<i>and indeed, for
international space travel.</i>

02:20:27.288 --> 02:20:29.290
I can't believe
anything you do works.

02:20:29.999 --> 02:20:31.167
Hey!

02:20:33.335 --> 02:20:34.336
Oh, God.

02:20:34.420 --> 02:20:36.005
I can't believe it.

02:20:37.214 --> 02:20:38.716
There's a little smell
going on over there, bud.

02:20:38.883 --> 02:20:42.261
I know. I haven't had a shower
in a year and a half.

02:20:42.636 --> 02:20:45.014
Don't make me laugh,
I have broken ribs.

02:21:19.089 --> 02:21:20.257
Hey, there.

02:21:28.807 --> 02:21:30.768
Morning, sir.
It's an honor, sir.

02:21:30.935 --> 02:21:31.936
Sir.

02:21:32.686 --> 02:21:34.355
Sir.
Sir.

02:21:34.605 --> 02:21:35.731
Morning, sir.

02:21:57.711 --> 02:21:59.713
<i>Welcome to the
Astronaut Candidate Program.</i>

02:22:00.506 --> 02:22:02.258
Now pay attention,

02:22:02.341 --> 02:22:03.968
because this could
save your life.

02:22:04.551 --> 02:22:06.220
Trust me, I know
what I'm talking about.

02:22:07.388 --> 02:22:09.473
Let me get a few things out
of the way, right off the bat.

02:22:09.640 --> 02:22:13.227
Yes, I did in fact survive
on a deserted planet

02:22:13.310 --> 02:22:15.354
by farming in my own shit.

02:22:16.563 --> 02:22:19.024
Yes, it's actually worse
than it sounds.

02:22:19.108 --> 02:22:20.859
So let's not talk
about that ever again.

02:22:22.236 --> 02:22:25.197
The other question I get
most frequently is...

02:22:25.364 --> 02:22:27.783
"When I was up there,
stranded by myself...

02:22:28.075 --> 02:22:30.411
"did I think
I was going to die?"

02:22:30.953 --> 02:22:32.454
Yes, absolutely.

02:22:33.622 --> 02:22:34.915
And that's what you need
to know going in

02:22:34.999 --> 02:22:36.667
because it's going
to happen to you.

02:22:37.084 --> 02:22:40.462
This is space.
It does not cooperate.

02:22:40.796 --> 02:22:43.549
At some point, everything is
going to go south on you.

02:22:43.716 --> 02:22:44.842
Everything is going
to go south

02:22:44.925 --> 02:22:46.427
and you're going to say,
"This is it.

02:22:46.719 --> 02:22:48.387
"This is how I end."

02:22:49.763 --> 02:22:51.307
Now, you can either
accept that...

02:22:51.849 --> 02:22:53.851
or you can get to work.

02:22:54.893 --> 02:22:56.603
That's all it is.

02:22:56.770 --> 02:22:58.605
You just begin.

02:22:59.440 --> 02:23:02.443
You do the math.
You solve one problem...

02:23:02.609 --> 02:23:04.153
then you solve the next one.

02:23:04.361 --> 02:23:05.362
And then the next.

02:23:05.487 --> 02:23:06.613
And if you solve
enough problems,

02:23:06.697 --> 02:23:08.324
you get to come home.

02:23:09.074 --> 02:23:11.118
All right, questions?

02:23:16.206 --> 02:23:17.583
<i>Once
Mission Control completes</i>

02:23:17.666 --> 02:23:18.751
<i>their pre-flight checks...</i>

02:23:18.876 --> 02:23:20.711
<i>we'll begin launch procedures.</i>

02:23:20.961 --> 02:23:23.422
<i>The</i> Ares 5 <i>team will
rendezvous with the</i> Hermes...

02:23:23.589 --> 02:23:26.216
approximately 48 minutes
after launch.

02:23:26.842 --> 02:23:28.844
From there,
they will have 414 days

02:23:28.927 --> 02:23:30.637
of space travel ahead of them.

02:23:30.804 --> 02:23:31.930
<i>Have the goals changed</i>

02:23:32.014 --> 02:23:33.515
<i>from the previous mission...</i>

02:23:33.682 --> 02:23:34.933
<i>to the</i> Ares 5 <i>program?</i>

02:23:35.100 --> 02:23:36.935
<i>And what do you hope to
achieve this time around?</i>

02:23:37.102 --> 02:23:38.896
<i>Well, no. The goals
have always been the same</i>

02:23:38.979 --> 02:23:40.439
for the <i>Ares</i> program.

02:23:41.357 --> 02:23:42.733
This time, of course,
we hope to bring

02:23:42.816 --> 02:23:44.068
all the astronauts
back at the same time.

02:23:45.319 --> 02:23:46.320
Flight,
Guidance check complete.

02:23:46.403 --> 02:23:47.404
Copy, Guidance.

02:23:48.822 --> 02:23:50.199
This is Flight.

02:23:50.366 --> 02:23:51.909
We are go for launch,
on schedule.

02:24:03.087 --> 02:24:04.171
<i>Final aerosurface</i>

02:24:04.254 --> 02:24:05.381
<i>checks are complete...</i>

02:24:05.547 --> 02:24:08.509
<i>as everything remains a go
for the launch of the</i> Ares 5.

02:24:09.218 --> 02:24:10.469
<i>20 seconds.</i>

02:24:11.845 --> 02:24:13.597
<i>Proceeding with the count.</i>

02:24:13.680 --> 02:24:15.974
<i>T-minus 10... 9...</i>

02:24:16.308 --> 02:24:17.559
<i>Main engine start.</i>

02:24:17.726 --> 02:24:21.105
<i>7... 6... 5... 4...</i>

02:24:21.688 --> 02:24:25.025
<i>three... two... one.</i>

02:24:25.192 --> 02:24:28.862
<i>And liftoff.
As the crew of the</i> Ares 5...

02:24:29.029 --> 02:24:32.699
<i>begin the next chapter
of American space exploration.</i>

02:24:32.908 --> 02:24:34.284
<i>Pressures look good.</i>

02:24:52.761 --> 02:24:54.346
Wow. Good shot.

02:24:54.513 --> 02:24:55.597
<i>There you have it.</i>

02:24:55.681 --> 02:24:58.100
<i>Five years after the rescue
of astronaut Mark Watney...</i>

02:24:58.267 --> 02:25:01.353
<i>an</i> Ares 5 <i>is on its way
to Mars.</i>

02:25:54.281 --> 02:25:55.741
<i>First I was afraid</i>

02:25:55.824 --> 02:25:57.451
<i>I was petrified</i>

02:25:57.701 --> 02:26:01.538
<i>Kept thinking I could never
live without you by my side</i>

02:26:01.788 --> 02:26:03.957
<i>But then I spent
so many nights</i>

02:26:04.041 --> 02:26:06.084
<i>Thinking how
you did me wrong</i>

02:26:06.168 --> 02:26:10.297
<i>And I grew strong
and I learned how to get along</i>

02:26:10.380 --> 02:26:13.634
<i>And so you're back
from outer space</i>

02:26:14.009 --> 02:26:15.844
<i>I just walked in
to find you here</i>

02:26:15.928 --> 02:26:17.971
<i>With that sad look
upon your face</i>

02:26:18.138 --> 02:26:20.516
<i>I should have changed
that stupid lock</i>

02:26:20.599 --> 02:26:22.309
<i>I should have made you
leave your key</i>

02:26:22.476 --> 02:26:24.603
<i>If I had known
for just one second</i>

02:26:24.686 --> 02:26:26.438
<i>You'd be back to bother me</i>

02:26:26.522 --> 02:26:29.983
<i>Boy,
now go, walk out the door</i>

02:26:30.567 --> 02:26:34.905
<i>Just turn around now 'cause
you're not welcome anymore</i>

02:26:35.822 --> 02:26:38.867
<i>Weren't you the one who
tried to hurt me with goodbye?</i>

02:26:38.951 --> 02:26:40.577
<i>Did you think I'd crumble?</i>

02:26:41.203 --> 02:26:42.829
<i>Did you think
I'd lay down and die?</i>

02:26:42.913 --> 02:26:46.708
<i>Oh, no, not I
I will survive</i>

02:26:47.042 --> 02:26:49.711
<i>For as long as
I know how to love</i>

02:26:49.836 --> 02:26:51.296
<i>I know I'll stay alive</i>

02:26:51.421 --> 02:26:53.215
<i>I've got all my life to live</i>

02:26:53.465 --> 02:26:55.342
<i>And I've got
all my love to give</i>

02:26:55.425 --> 02:26:56.885
<i>And I'll survive</i>

02:26:57.344 --> 02:26:58.845
<i>I will survive</i>

